.TH KOSH 1 "2026-08-18" "kosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kosh \- a fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell,
formatter, and language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kosh
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-s
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-c
.IR command ...]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-format
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-lint
.RI [ \-\-format ]
.B \-\-apply
.I file ...
.br
.B kosh
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kosh
is the Koshka shell executable. It is an interpreter, an interactive shell, a
formatter, a language server, and a diagnostic tool. It accepts full Bash and
POSIX shell syntax with Bash-compatible extensions.
.PP
Koshka supports Linux, macOS, and Windows with the same grammar. An unquoted
backslash is an escape on Windows. A native Windows path is written with a
quoted backslash, an escaped backslash, or a forward slash.
.SH INVOCATION
The input source is selected in this order. The
.B \-s
option selects standard input. One or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings. A script operand selects that file. The
.B \-i
option or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor.
Otherwise, standard input is used.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-lint
analyzes each command string in the order given, followed by every file
operand. With no command string and no file operand, standard input is
analyzed.
.PP
The
.B \-\-format
option formats standard input when no file is given, and otherwise formats one
file to standard output. When
.B \-\-lint
and
.B \-\-format
are combined, the lint fixes are applied first and the result is then
formatted.
.PP
The
.B \-s
option takes precedence over command strings and file operands. Command strings
and a script operand take precedence over
.BR \-i .
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood. The basename
.B sh
or
.B dash
selects the
.B sh
mood. The basename
.B bash
or
.B rbash
selects the
.B bash
mood. The basename
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero or the
.B \-l
option selects login operation.
.PP
The
.B KOSH_FLAGS
environment variable supplies options before the command line is parsed. A
command-line option overrides the corresponding value from
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
The options
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-format ,
.BR \-\-apply ,
.BR \-\-as\-language\-server ,
.BR \-\-rcfile ,
.BR \-\-init\-file ,
the debug options, and every operand are ignored in
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
A secure execution ignores and removes
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.PP
The shell enters rescue mode when argument zero begins with a dash, its options
cannot be parsed, and standard input is a terminal. Rescue mode clears the
options and skips startup files. An option that requests restricted mode still
makes the rescue shell restricted. Any other invalid invocation exits with
status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version and notices are displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
The short version is displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Help is displayed. The shell then exits.
.SS POSIX options
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
The shell runs interactively.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Commands are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fICOMMAND\fR ", " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
COMMAND is run. The option may be repeated, and the command strings run in
the order given. A command string with a syntax error ends the shell with
status 2, one that analysis rejects ends it with status 1, and the later strings
do not run.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
The shell exits after an unguarded failing command.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Pathname expansion is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
One command is taken from input and run. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Each input line is printed as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Each command is printed after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Each later assignment is marked for export.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
A plain output redirection does not overwrite an existing regular file.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Commands are parsed and analyzed without being run. A compatibility mood
reports only the tiers selected by
.BR \-W .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
Expanding an unset variable is an error.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
The shell acts as a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-m
The options are accepted without effect.
.TP
.BR \-o " " \fINAME\fR ", " +o " " \fINAME\fR
The option NAME is enabled by
.B \-o
and disabled by
.BR +o ,
as with
.B set \-o
.I NAME
and
.B set +o
.IR NAME .
.B \-o posix
acts as
.BR \-\-posix .
An unknown option exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR +a ", " +C ", " +e ", " +f ", " +n ", " +p ", " +t ", " +u ", " +v ", " +x
The letter's option is turned off, as with
.BR "set +e" .
.PP
An option given on the command line, on or off, overrides the settings files
and KOSHCONF.
.TP
.B \-\-
Option parsing ends, and the next operand is the script.
.SS Bash options
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-rcfile " " \fIFILE\fR
FILE is the interactive rc file. The file replaces ~/.bashrc in an
interactive non-login Bash mood, and the user rc script in an interactive
non-login
.B kosh
mood.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
The interactive non-login Bash rc, the user rc script, and a custom rc file are
skipped. Login profiles and BASH_ENV processing are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-O " " \fINAME\fR ", " +O " " \fINAME\fR
The shopt option NAME is turned on, or off with
.BR +O ,
as with
.B shopt \-s
.I NAME
and
.B shopt \-u
.IR NAME ,
and overrides the settings files. An unknown option exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
The shell becomes restricted after the startup files finish.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Privileged mode is enabled. BASH_ENV is not sourced, KOSHCONF from the
environment is ignored and removed, and the settings files are not read. A
secure execution, such as a setuid or setgid invocation, always drops the
elevated ids and skips every startup file and settings file.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
The bash-posix mood is selected.
.SS Compatibility options
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
NAME is
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.BR bash-posix .
For a script file operand, the first line selects the mood. A bash shebang
selects
.BR bash ,
an sh or dash shebang selects
.BR sh ,
and a kosh shebang selects
.BR kosh .
The interpreter may be given through
.BR env .
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the
.B kosh
mood. An explicit
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
or an invocation basename such as sh or bash takes precedence over the shebang.
The shebang is not used for
.BR \-c ,
standard input, or
.BR \-\-lint .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Startup files for each listed mood are sourced in order. LIST is a
comma-separated list of moods. When the option is omitted, an interactive or
login shell uses init_moods from the configuration, and any other shell uses
the selected runtime mood.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-enable\-mimicry
A script run as a command with a recognized shell shebang runs under the
corresponding mood. A script operand always does, with or without this
option. The precedence rules of
.B \-\-mood
apply.
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
The options
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-\-no\-completion ,
.BR "\-\-tab\-selector plain" ,
and
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
are applied. Color is disabled. An explicit
.B \-\-tab\-selector
overrides the plain selector.
.SS Auxiliary options
.TP
.B \-\-lint
The option behaves like
.B \-n
with every diagnostic tier enabled at its normal severity, overriding
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
and
.BR \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics .
Every startup file is skipped.
.PP
Shell commands are extracted from CI configurations, Ansible, Dockerfile,
Compose, Markdown, Makefile, justfile, Kubernetes, and similar hosts. Only the
extracted sh, bash, or kosh source is parsed and diagnosed. A host interpreter
setting, such as a step shell, a SHELL assignment or instruction, a shebang, or
an executable option, selects the shell, and a command for another interpreter
is skipped. An exec array contributes only the script of a shell
.B \-c
command. Each justfile recipe line, joined across backslash continuations, is
checked as one command, and a shebang or script recipe is checked as one
script. A Jinja template of a shell script, such as
.IR name .sh.j2,
is checked with its template syntax masked. Host template expressions and
editor variables are masked in every format. Interpolated values are treated as
plain words, and a command that cannot be resolved is not reported in a
fragment that contains an interpolation. Variables that the host provides, such
as CI variables, exported recipe parameters, and Dockerfile ARG and ENV values,
are treated as set. Diagnostics point into the host file and show its text.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
safe structured fixes are written to each file operand. The updated source is
analyzed again, and only the remaining diagnostics are reported. A warning-only
result exits successfully. A remaining error or an input or write failure exits
with status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-format
Shell source is formatted without being run. Structural keywords and compound
braces are placed on separate lines. Each nested level uses two spaces. Direct
.B test
commands become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
them. Code is wrapped at 80 columns when a safe token boundary exists.
Output uses LF line endings. With no file or a lone
.B \-
operand, standard input is formatted.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
File operands are updated with safe lint fixes under
.BR \-\-lint ,
or with formatted source under
.BR \-\-format .
The two modes may be combined. A changed file is replaced atomically, and file
modes and symbolic links are retained. Standard input,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-c ,
and a lone
.B \-
operand are rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
The language server protocol is run over standard input and standard output.
The server can provide diagnostics, quick fixes, completion, navigation, command
information, semantic tokens, an outline, and rename. A fix-all action applies
every non-conflicting safe fix in the open document.
.PP
The server recognizes embedded shell regions in the host formats described
under
.BR \-\-lint ,
and language features operate only inside them. Rename updates variables,
functions, and aliases throughout the open document. A reference written as
.B $name
or
.B ${name}
keeps its sigils and braces, and only the variable is replaced. A command can
be renamed only when the document defines it as a function or alias. The mood
is selected by a recognized shebang, then the client language identifier, then
a recognized document extension. An unrecognized document uses the
.B kosh
mood. This option does not accept file operands,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
or
.BR \-\-apply .
.SS Koshka options
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-no\-init\-files
No startup file is read. PATH is set to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
No settings file is read, and KOSHCONF from the environment is ignored and
removed. PATH and the shell startup files are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-WW ", " \-WWW
Analysis has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers. In the default mood, strict and lenient diagnostics are errors, while annoying diagnostics are
warnings. The
.B \-W
form retains those severities,
.B \-WW
demotes lenient errors to warnings, and
.B \-WWW
also demotes strict errors. In other moods,
.B \-W
enables strict diagnostics as warnings,
.B \-WW
also enables lenient diagnostics, and
.B \-WWW
also enables annoying diagnostics. In the Bash and POSIX
moods no analysis runs until a level is set. A level changed with
.B set
is inherited by launched shells through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS .
Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
The available analysis checks and their metadata are listed. The shell then
exits.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
Analysis is skipped, and no warning or pre-run diagnostic is reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
The annoying tier is suppressed. Strict and lenient analysis is unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Diagnostics and warnings are suppressed while startup files are sourced. The
previous settings are restored before interactive input begins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Source backtraces are suppressed. This turns optimizer.show_source_traces off.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Tab completion, ghost text, and syntax coloring are disabled. This turns
completion.on_tab off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Syntax coloring and ghost text are disabled. Tab completion remains enabled.
This turns editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text off.
.TP
.BR \-\-tab\-selector " " \fIMODE\fR
MODE selects how an explicit Tab presents several candidates and how Ctrl\-R
presents the history. Valid modes are
.BR interactive ,
.BR external ,
and
.BR plain .
The default is
.BR interactive .
The behavior of each mode is described under
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-koshkit
Bundled utilities resolve directly as commands. The option is equivalent to
.BR "koshconf set interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands on" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic
Arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values are enabled. The option
is equivalent to
.BR "koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers on" .
The
.B kosh
mood keeps this option on.
.SS Debug options
Only a debug build accepts
.B \-X
and the options beginning with
.BR \-\-debug\- .
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
Syntax trees are printed before execution and during formatting or linting.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics
Each constant-folded expression and eliminated branch is reported as a lenient
analysis diagnostic. A compatibility mood reports these diagnostics only under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-exit\-code
A located diagnostic is printed when a command returns a non-zero status. The
.B "koshconf set debug.report_nonzero_exit_codes on"
form turns the diagnostic on while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
Every command status is reported, including a successful zero. The
.BR "set \-N" " and " "set +N"
forms turn the diagnostic on and off while the shell runs.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
The escape bitmap of each parsed command is printed.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Statistics on commands, processes, input and output, and resource usage are
printed after each command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Memory usage is printed at exit.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-debug\-logging =\fILEVEL\fR
Internal logging is enabled at
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
or
.BR all .
An unknown level is a usage error.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-logging\-file =\fIFILE\fR
Debug logging is appended to FILE. Standard error is used when the option is
omitted.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-complete\-at =\fILINE\fR
Completion candidates for LINE are printed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-highlight\-at =\fILINE\fR
Highlight spans for LINE are printed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-ghost\-at =\fILINE\fR
The ghost completion result for LINE is printed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-brackets\-at =\fILINE\fR
The bracket pair matched at each caret offset of LINE is printed. The shell
then exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-hint\-at =\fILINE\fR
The inline hint row for LINE is printed with the caret at its end. The shell
then exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-highlight\-restarts\-at =\fITEXT\fR
TEXT is highlighted from each of its restart points, and the number of restart
points and of scans that color the rest differently from a scan from the
start are printed. The shell then exits with status 1 when any scan differs.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Function or system-call tracing is enabled. These options are available only in
a Cosmopolitan build.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand is the script file. A lone
.B \-
operand selects standard input. A directory operand fails with status 126. A
file that cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script invocation, the script pathname becomes $0. Arguments after the
script become positional parameters. For a
.B \-c
invocation, the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. Later operands become
positional parameters. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 remains the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.SH MOODS
The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis disabled by default. The
.B sh
mood follows the supported dash behavior. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled and sources the Bash startup
files.
.PP
The
.B kosh
mood enables strict checks and analysis by default. It holds nounset, pipefail,
failglob, and extended arithmetic on, and nullglob off.
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
and
.B koshconf
report an error for any other value and suggest
.BR "koshconf set mood bash" ,
but accept the value already held, so
.B set \-euo pipefail
still runs. Every other option that changes evaluation can be toggled. In the
.BR bash ,
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B sh
moods, every Bash option can be toggled through
.B set
and
.BR shopt .
An explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
.BR "shopt \-s failglob" ,
or
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
in such a mood survives a later change between those moods. The command
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix .
The command
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
only from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
An error that ends a non-interactive shell in every mood, such as an unset
parameter expanded under
.B set \-u
or ${name?word} with name unset, passes through an eval and a sourced file, as
it does in Bash and dash. A subshell, a command substitution, a trap action,
and a startup file hold it.
.PP
On Windows, the default mood leaves backslashes literal in
.BR echo .
The
.B \-e
option enables escape decoding. The
.B sh
mood retains dash-style default escape handling.
.PP
On Windows, a path of
.B /dev/null
opens the null device, and a path of
.B /tmp
or a path under it maps to the same path under the temporary directory held
in
.BR TEMP .
The options
.B compatibility.dev_null
and
.BR compatibility.slash_tmp ,
both on by default, govern the two mappings. Other platforms keep both paths
unchanged.
.SH OPTION REGISTRY
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR koshconf ,
and the
.B \-o
test share one registry. Each option has a numeric id, a koshconf name, a type,
a class, and, when it has them, a Bash spelling and a letter. An interactive
option affects only the editor, the history, or the presentation of
diagnostics. Every other option is semantic
and can change what a script does or writes.
.PP
A koshconf name starts with its topic. The topics are
.B mood
and
.B init_moods
(mood selection),
.B interpreter
(behavior of the shell itself),
.B compatibility
(Windows mapping of POSIX paths),
.B editor
(the line editor, with the subtopics
.B editor.history
and
.BR editor.hints ),
.BR completion ,
.BR debug ,
and
.B optimizer
(the analysis stage and its reports).
.B koshconf list
shows the current value of each option.
.PP
The canonical name of an option inherited from POSIX
.B set \-o
starts with posix, and one inherited from any other Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option starts with bash, as in posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure for
errexit and bash.interpreter.glob_extended_patterns for extglob. Any other
option exists only in Koshka.
.PP
Every option with a Bash spelling is also accepted under an alias. The
alias is posix.legacy.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME, otherwise bash.legacy.NAME when
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
accepts NAME, as in bash.legacy.extglob and bash.legacy.posix. An alias is the
same option as the canonical name, so setting either spelling changes the value
shown by the other. A kosh.conf line, koshconf set, koshconf get, and every
option operand accept the alias.
.B koshconf create
and
.BR "koshconf list" ,
error messages, and KOSHCONF use only the canonical name, except that
.B koshconf list \-\-all
adds each alias on a line of its own.
.PP
A configuration file may set every option except those fixed by the
invocation or dependent on the session. bash.interpreter.login_shell and
bash.interpreter.restricted_shell are read-only.
bash.interpreter.privileged_mode, posix.interpreter.parse_without_executing, and
bash.interpreter.exit_after_one_command come from the command line.
posix.interpreter.job_control, bash.editor.history.records_commands,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, and bash.interpreter.posixly_correct follow
the session and the mood.
.PP
editor.base_mode is one option that covers the Bash options emacs and vi. Its
value is vi while
.B set \-o vi
is on and emacs otherwise.
.BR "set \-o emacs" ,
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
their
.B +o
forms, the
.B \-o
test, and SHELLOPTS keep the Bash behavior.
.PP
The Bash options hashall and nolog have no koshconf name.
.B set
and
.B \-o
accept both for Bash scripts that query them.
.PP
The shopt options with a bash.legacy prefix in the table are accepted and never
change behavior, so Bash startup files that set them run unchanged. Koshka
always appends to the history file and records a multi-line command as one
entry. shopt accepts array_expand_once as the Bash 5.3 spelling of
assoc_expand_once.
.PP
