.PP
The mood option takes one of kosh, sh, bash, and bash-posix.
completion.menu_style takes one of interactive, external, and plain.
optimizer.warning_level takes one of 0, 1, 2, and 3. editor.base_mode takes
emacs or vi. editor.history.file_path and editor.history.max_entries are the
KOSH_HISTORY_FILE and KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE variables. editor.wrap_width is a
decimal integer from 0 to 65535, 80 by default. init_moods is a
comma-separated list of the moods accepted by
.BR \-L ,
empty by default. It applies only to an interactive or login shell at startup
and yields to
.BR \-L .
A restricted shell ignores it in the configuration files. completion.on_tab,
editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text, and optimizer.show_source_traces
are the settings for
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-traces .
.SH STARTUP FILES
Every shell applies the configuration files before any shell startup file.
These are /etc/kosh.conf (%ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf on Windows), then
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf (~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf when XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, empty, or relative). The system file is skipped with a warning unless
only the system administrator can change it. The files are not shell code.
.BR kosh (5)
describes their format. A
.B KOSHCONF
variable is applied after them and removed from the environment. A later
setting replaces an earlier one, and a malformed line is reported and skipped.
A mood chosen by the configuration selects the startup files below unless the
command line or the invocation name selects one. Options given on the command
line keep their values.
.PP
A login shell and an interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood source ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh, and a Bash startup file that
the script sources runs in the
.B bash
mood, as described in
.BR kosh (5).
No system rc file is sourced.
A
.B kosh
session started with
.B \-L bash
sources the bash startup files in the
.B bash
mood and then returns to the
.B kosh
mood. The init_moods option in a configuration file does the same for
every interactive or login shell started without
.BR \-L .
Every mood sets KOSH without exporting it, so a line guarded with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile in that order. An interactive shell in
the
.B sh
mood sources the file in ENV, used as a pathname without PATH lookup, unless
privileged mode is on.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile, then the first readable file among
~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile.
.PP
An interactive non-login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources the first readable file among /etc/bash/bashrc and
/etc/bash.bashrc, then the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc, then
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion.
.PP
A noninteractive shell in the
.B bash
mood sources BASH_ENV once, without a PATH search. A shell in the
.B bash-posix
mood does not source BASH_ENV.
.PP
Clean, rescue, lint, format, and language server shells skip every startup and
configuration file, as do
.B \-Q
and a shell whose real and effective identities differ. The privileged option
suppresses ENV and BASH_ENV processing. Restricted mode and the strictness of the
selected mood take effect after startup finishes.
.PP
Missing startup files are skipped. An unreadable custom rc or BASH_ENV file
also produces a diagnostic.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes, changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV, and
BASH_ENV, a directory separator in a command word or a source operand, every
use of
.BR "hash \-p" ,
and every file operand of
.BR history .
.PP
A restricted shell also rejects the redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and
>&, the
.BR exec ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR bench ,
and
.B assimilate
builtins,
.BR "command \-p" ,
.BR "enable \-f" ,
.BR "enable \-d" ,
.BR "set \-M" ,
.BR "set \-I" ,
.BR "set +I" ,
.BR "set +r" ,
and every form of the
.B koshkit
builtin. Numeric descriptor duplication remains available. A command word falls
back only to a bundled utility that a startup file enabled with
.BR enable .
After startup,
.B enable
can disable a builtin but cannot enable a disabled builtin or change a bundled
utility. An enabled utility that starts other programs, such as
.BR env ,
.BR xargs ,
.BR find ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR timeout ,
or
.BR watch ,
runs any program its arguments give, including another shell, so a startup
file for a restricted shell should enable only utilities that start no program.
.PP
The
.B koshconf
forms
.BR create ,
.BR set ,
and
.B load
are rejected, so a restricted shell cannot change a setting or write a
configuration file.
.B get
and
.B list
remain available. No setting can turn restricted mode off. A shell started
restricted by
.B \-r
or the invocation name rbash ignores and removes
.B KOSHCONF
with a warning. The configuration files apply only presentation and diagnostic
options. When a file sets the mood, a semantic option,
editor.history.file_path, or editor.history.max_entries, the shell warns and
ignores it. A child shell inherits the restrictions, and a sourced script
retains them. A shell script found through PATH and executed as a command runs
without them.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described below. The
.B sh
mood disables Bash-only syntax except for documented compatibility forms.
Differences are listed under
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
An if command with no selected branch and no else command returns status zero.
.SS Coprocesses
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
Every mood except
.B sh
accepts a coprocess. The command runs asynchronously and two pipes connect
it to the shell. NAME[0] is read from, NAME[1] is written to, and NAME_PID holds
the process id. The default NAME is COPROC. A word before the command is the
identifier only when a compound command follows it. The launch returns status
zero.
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve every enclosed byte. Double quotes retain parameter,
command, and arithmetic expansion. A backslash quotes the next byte where the
grammar permits it.
.PP
The form $'...' interprets C escapes including \en, \et, \exHH, and \eOOO. The
form
$"..." marks a string for translation and behaves as an ordinary double-quoted
string without a catalog.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional parameters
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. The form "$@" preserves one
field per positional parameter. The form "$*" joins the parameters with the
first byte of IFS. In the sh mood, as in dash, $@ and $* are always set, so
"${@\-d}" and "${@+p}" never test for missing parameters. A $@ that becomes one
string, as in an assignment, joins the parameters with the first byte of IFS in
the sh mood and with a space in the bash moods.
.SS Indexed arrays
An indexed array is assigned with a=(x y z), an element with a[2]=z, and
a+=(w) appends. ${a[i]} takes an arithmetic subscript.
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list elements. ${#a[@]} counts elements. ${!a[@]} lists
indices, omitting absent sparse ones. A negative index counts from the end.
.PP
Inside double quotes, "${a[@]}" preserves one field per element. The form
"${a[@]@A}" yields the words of the declaration. "${a[*]}" joins elements with
the first byte of IFS. An unquoted ${a[@]} or ${a[*]} joins elements with the
first byte of IFS and splits the result. An unset array in ${a[@]} is an error
under
.BR "set \-u" .
.SS Associative arrays
The command
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array. The form m[key]=v assigns an element. The form
${m[key]} expands to it. The form ${!m[@]} lists keys. The command
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one element.
.PP
The key order of an associative array is unspecified and may differ from Bash.
The shell lists keys in the order first assigned, and a script that depends on
an order should sort the keys.
.PP
The
.B export
and
.B declare \-x
commands on an indexed or associative array keep the array and record the
export attribute, which
.B declare \-p
prints as \-ax or \-Ax. As in Bash, no child process receives the array in its
environment.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.SS Parameter expansion
The form ${v:offset:length} selects a substring. A negative offset requires a
leading space, as in ${v: \-3}. Without the space, ${v:\-3} is the default-value
form. A negative length excludes that many characters from the end.
On an indexed array, ${a[@]:offset:length} starts at the first element whose
index is at least the offset and takes length elements.
.PP
The form ${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match. The form ${v//pat/rep} replaces
every match. The anchors /# and /% select the start and end. The
.B patsub_replacement
shopt option is enabled by default. While enabled, an unquoted ampersand in the
replacement denotes the matched text, and so does an ampersand from an unquoted
expansion. A quoted ampersand and the form \e& denote a literal ampersand. When
the option is disabled, every ampersand is literal.
.PP
The forms ${v^^}, ${v,,}, ${v^}, and ${v,} change case with an optional pattern.
The transforms ${v@Q}, ${v@E}, ${v@A}, ${v@P}, and ${v@a} produce a quoted
value, expanded escapes, an assignment, a prompt expansion, or attributes.
A value or key with a byte that does not print in the locale, such as a
control byte or invalid UTF-8, takes the $'...' form in ${v@Q}, ${v@A},
${a[@]@K}, printf %q, declare \-p, and export \-p. The forms ${v@U}, ${v@L},
and ${v@u} apply the corresponding case conversion. Any other operator after @
on a set parameter is a bad substitution that ends the script.
.PP
The form ${!ref} expands the variable whose identifier is in ref. The forms
${!pre*} and ${!pre@} list variables beginning with pre.
.PP
When ${v:\-word}, ${v:+word}, or ${v:=word}, with or without the colon, sits
in double quotes or a here-document, the word follows double-quote rules.
Single quotes in it stay literal, so "${v:\-'a'}" expands to 'a' with its
quotes.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
Arithmetic accepts the C operators, ++, \-\-, **, the conditional operator, the
comma operator, and assignment operators. Literals may use 0x hexadecimal, 0
octal, 0b binary, or a base prefix such as 16#ff. A variable value is
re-evaluated as an arithmetic expression without further expansion, so a $ or a
backquote in it is an error. A subscript taken from a variable value is
expanded again, as in Bash, so q='a[$i]' and $((q)) yield the element at i. An
empty (( )) command returns one. An empty $(( )) expansion produces zero.
Outside the sh mood, double quotes in the text of $(( )), (( )), a C-style for
header, a subscript, or a substring offset are removed before the evaluation,
so $(( "1 + 2" * 3 )) is 7. The sh mood rejects every quote.
.PP
Outside the sh mood, $[expression] is the obsolete Bash spelling of
$((expression)). The sh mood keeps $[ literal. Analysis reports the spelling
as SC2007.
.PP
An arithmetic error in the bash and bash-posix moods abandons the rest of the
input line, as in Bash. This covers a $(( )) expansion, a subscript, a substring
offset, and an assignment to an integer variable. Every command left on the line
stops, and $? is 1 on the next line. Neither set \-e nor an ERR trap acts on it.
An eval, a sourced file, a subshell, and a command substitution are separate
lines. An error in the expression of (( )),
.BR let ,
a [[ ]] operand, or a C-style for header fails only that command with status 1.
.PP
A $(( )) error outside a here-document body ends a non-interactive shell with
status 1 in the bash-posix mood and 2 in the sh mood, as in Bash POSIX mode and
dash. An interactive shell abandons the line instead. In the kosh mood every
arithmetic error ends the script with status 1.
.PP
Other expansion errors follow Bash 5.3. A glob matching nothing under failglob
abandons the line in the bash and bash-posix moods. A bad substitution, such as
${x!y} or ${x:}, and an assignment to a read-only variable abandon the line in
the bash mood and end a non-interactive shell in the bash-posix and sh moods.
An unset parameter under set \-u and ${NAME?word} end the script in every mood.
In the kosh mood each of these errors ends the script.
.PP
In the sh mood, an error of a regular builtin, such as a missing cd directory or
an invalid test operand, fails that command with status 2 and the script goes
on. An error of a special builtin or of local, such as shift past the last
parameter or export of an invalid identifier, ends a non-interactive shell with
status 2 unless command runs it. In every mood printf without a format fails
with status 2.
.PP
The
.B interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
koshconf option selects arbitrary-precision integers and finite base-10 decimal
literals. The default mood always holds it on, and the other moods start with it
off. When it is disabled, signed 64-bit arithmetic wraps.
.SS Command substitution
The forms $(command) and `command` replace a command with its output. A command
substitution restores runtime state, shell options, directory stack, working
directory state, and file creation mask when it finishes. Trailing newlines are
removed from the output. The bash and sh moods also drop every null byte, and
the kosh mood keeps them. A syntax error inside a command, process, or function
substitution is reported at its place in the outer source, and a script or
command string containing one does not run. A substitution nested more than 64
levels deep is a syntax error.
.PP
Analysis checks the commands inside substitutions and reports findings at the
line and column of the file. The body of a command, backtick, or process
substitution is analyzed as a subshell, and a function substitution shares the
current shell.
.SS Process substitution
The forms <(command) and >(command) supply a descriptor path. The command runs
in a separate process. A form expands anywhere in an unquoted word, so
p=<(command) assigns the path and x<(command) joins it to the text around it.
The path stays open until the command that expanded it finishes, and a path in
the word list of a for loop stays open until the loop finishes. The shell then
closes it and waits for the process, while Bash leaves the process running.
A process substitution sets
.B $!
to its process, and
.B wait
with that process returns its status. On POSIX the path is /dev/fd/63 or the
next free descriptor above it. On Windows the path is a pipe that behaves like
a FIFO. Each reader continues where the previous reader stopped, and each
writer appends to the same stream.
.SS Brace expansion
The form {a,b,c} produces alternatives. The forms {1..10}, {a..z}, and
{1..10..2} produce ranges. A numeric range such as {001..10} retains its width.
Braces nest. A lone {a} remains literal.
.SS Field splitting
IFS controls field splitting. A run of an IFS whitespace byte collapses. A run
of a non-whitespace IFS byte produces an empty field where applicable.
.SS Pathname expansion
The bash moods sort the matches of a pattern by the collation order of the
locale, as Bash does. The kosh and sh moods sort them by byte value.
.PP
The
.B extglob
option enables ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !() matching. The pattern of
.B [[
always matches these groups, whatever the option. The
.B globstar
option enables **. The
.BR dotglob ,
.BR nullglob ,
and
.B failglob
options control leading dots, nonmatches, and failed matches. In the default
mood a nonmatching glob is fatal. In a compatibility mood with failglob
disabled, the nonmatching pattern remains literal.
.PP
A nonempty
.B GLOBIGNORE
holds colon\-separated patterns. A pathname expansion result matching one
pattern is removed, and a glob whose results are all removed follows the
nonmatching rules. The entries . and .. are never results while GLOBIGNORE is
nonempty. A nonempty assignment enables the
.B dotglob
option, and an unset of GLOBIGNORE disables it.
.PP
The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG selects the character
locale. A value whose codeset is UTF\-8 or utf8 selects UTF\-8 when the platform
supports that locale. Any other value, an empty value, and no value select
bytes.
.PP
In UTF\-8, a ? and each step of a * consume one whole character, a bracket
expression compares code points, ${#v} and ${v:offset:length} count
characters, and ${v^}, ${v,}, and ${v~} change the case of multibyte letters.
In bytes, each of them uses one byte. The rule applies to pathname expansion,
case, [[ == ]], ${v#pat}, ${v%pat}, ${v/pat/rep}, ${v^pat}, and compgen \-X.
Completion always matches by UTF\-8 sequence.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
The operators &> and &>> redirect standard output and standard error to one
file. The operator |& pipes both streams. The operator <<< supplies a
here-string followed by one newline. A descriptor number written before <<<
or <<, as in 7<<< or 8<<EOF, sets the descriptor that receives the input.
.PP
A redirection of the form {var}>, {var}<, {var}<<<, or {var}<< allocates a
descriptor numbered ten or greater and assigns its number to var. The form
{var}>&\- closes the descriptor whose number var holds.
.PP
An appending redirection inside a loop body opens its file once and keeps the
descriptor until the loop ends. Writes continue to the original file even after
it is renamed or removed. Bash reopens the file on every iteration.
.PP
In the bash moods, the redirections of a program, of a command with no word,
and of a subshell expand in a child process, as in Bash. A side effect of such
an expansion, such as ${v:=x}, leaves no change in the shell. The redirections
of a builtin, a function, a command group, and
.B exec
expand in the shell itself, and so do those of every command in the kosh mood.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Command lookup checks functions, enabled builtins, cached program paths, PATH,
and bundled koshkit commands when the active mood, the options, or the
.B enable
builtin allow them. Running a
command or using the
.B hash
builtin caches its resolved program path. A change to the contents of any PATH
directory clears every cached path.
.B hash \-r
forgets cached command locations.
.B hash \-R
forgets them and searches PATH again immediately.
.PP
In an interactive shell, a single directory path in command position changes
the working directory as if it were an operand to
.BR cd .
A script is never affected, as in bash.
Functions, builtins, programs, and bundled utilities take precedence. The
.B autocd
shopt option controls this behavior and is enabled by default in every mood.
When the command consists only of dots and is a single word, two dots move to
the parent directory and each additional dot moves one more parent directory.
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when the
target is not a directory.
.PP
A script without a recognized executable format is run by the shell itself.
The caller's variables, functions, and traps are not inherited.
.PP
PIPESTATUS, updated after every foreground command, holds the status of each
pipeline stage. When
.B pipefail
is enabled, a pipeline returns the status of its rightmost failing stage.
A pipeline stage expanding to no command, such as
.BR "p=1 | cat" ,
is an error in the kosh mood. The other moods run it as an empty command
that applies its redirections and returns 0.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function has its own positional parameters and local variables. FUNCNAME,
BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND expose the call stack.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation inherits that
condition, and
.B errexit
stays suppressed throughout its body.
.PP
In the default mood the RETURN trap fires at the boundary of every function
body. The
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods fire it for a body that the
.B functrace
option follows. The
.B sh
mood rejects the RETURN condition.
.SH BUILTINS
The shell provides the POSIX and Bash builtins listed below. The standard forms
follow the active compatibility mood unless a difference is stated here.
.PP
With no operand,
.B builtin
groups the builtins under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings. The Koshka section
holds assimilate, bench, koshconf, koshkit, slurp, and z.
.PP
\fB: . [ alias bg bind break builtin caller cd command complete compgen compopt
continue declare dirs disown echo enable eval exec exit export false fc fg
getopts hash help history jobs kill let local logout mapfile newgrp popd printf
pushd pwd read readarray readonly return set shift shopt source suspend test
time times trap true type typeset ulimit umask unalias unset wait\fR
.PP
.B pwd \-W
prints the physical working directory in the Windows form with forward slashes,
as MSYS does. On other systems, it is the same as
.BR "pwd \-P" .
.PP
.BI "source \-\-mood " "MOOD FILE"
and
.BI "source \-\-mood=" "MOOD FILE"
run FILE in MOOD, one of kosh, bash, sh, and bash\-posix, instead of the mood
detected from its shebang or name, and return to the previous mood afterwards.
A startup file of another shell that rc.kosh sources this way runs without the
diagnostics of the
.B kosh
mood. A restricted shell rejects
.BR \-\-mood .
.SS Shell-specific builtins
.TP
.BI "assimilate " TARGET
The running executable is copied through scp to TARGET and installed as
.B kosh
in the first usable remote PATH directory. A path containing sbin is
skipped. The
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
option requests a relative bash, dash, sh, or kosh link. It may be repeated and
accepts comma-separated values. The
.BI \-\-install\-directory= DIR
option installs into DIR instead, even when it contains sbin.
.PP
The shell asks for confirmation with the target and the full path of the new
binary. Only an answer that begins with y or Y continues, and any other answer
exits with status 1. The
.BR \-y " or " \-\-yes
option skips the question. The
.BR \-x " or " \-\-trace
option prints remote progress. The
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
options replace ssh and scp.
.PP
The uploaded candidate is validated before the target is replaced. A failed
installation restores the previous file or symlink.
.TP
.B bench
Each command is run through
.BR "kosh \-c" ,
and the wall time, peak resident set, and available performance counters are
reported as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Sampling stops after a
nonzero status unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is present. The
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-N " (" \-\-no\-shell ),
.BR \-n " (" \-\-runs ),
and
.BR \-t " (" \-\-duration )
options control output, direct execution, sample count, and the millisecond
budget.
.BR \-w " (" \-\-warmup )
sets the number of unmeasured runs before sampling. Without a value it runs
one to three, stopping when two runs in a row agree within 10%. Without the
option one unmeasured run precedes sampling. A note
follows the report when the first sample, or any sample, is an outlier by a
modified z-score above 14 against the median absolute deviation of the wall
times.
.BR \-p " (" \-\-profile )
takes one extended sample, the same as
.BR "\-\-runs 1 \-\-extended" .
With
.BR \-E " (" \-\-extended ),
each command also reports CPU time, CPU usage over wall time, waiting time
(wall time minus user and system time) and its share of wall time, the
system share of CPU time, context switches and their involuntary share, page
faults, block operations, and read and write calls and bytes. When the traced
run finds more than one active task on average, waiting time is the average
active task count times wall time, minus user and system time, so idle threads
of a parallel command count as waiting. Linux adds
scheduler wait, time slices, CPU migrations, cancelled writes, and the branch,
stall, cache, and TLB hardware counters with instructions per cycle and the
miss ratios. Linux also reports sleep, block, and IO wait times when kernel
scheduler statistics are enabled. macOS adds cycles, instructions,
performance-core counters, run-queue wait, wakeups, energy, page-ins, peak
footprint, storage bytes, and logical writes. Windows counts the whole process
tree through a job object and adds peak commit, other IO calls, and the number
of processes. The extended hardware counters are sampled on alternate runs.
On Linux, one
extra untimed run under ptrace counts system calls, started threads and
processes, execs, the peak number of tasks, and the average number of active
tasks, where a task that waits for a child counts as idle. Thread efficiency
is CPU usage divided by that average. These rows are marked (traced). Run as
root, Linux counts system calls on every run, and macOS samples the processor
counters system-wide. A row marked * counts only the measured process, not the
processes it started. A row the platform cannot report is omitted.
.TP
.BI "koshconf create " "PRESET " "\fR[\fB\-\-force\fR]"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI "koshconf set " "OPTION VALUE " "\fR[\fB\-\-persist\fR]"
.TP
.BI "koshconf get " OPTION
.TP
.BR "koshconf list " "[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR]"
.TP
.BI "koshconf load " ENCODED
.TP
.BR "koshconf save " "[\fB\-\-persist\fR]"
.PD
The koshconf builtin is the only interface to the settings Koshka owns.
.IP
.B create
writes the default file of the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
or
.B sh
preset to the user configuration file described in
.BR kosh (5),
and refuses to replace an existing file without
.BR \-\-force .
It also writes the default user rc script when that script is missing.
.B set
changes the option in the current session. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
it also rewrites that option's line in the user configuration file and leaves
every other line in place. The previous file is saved as
.IR kosh.conf.bak .
.B get
prints the current value.
.B list
prints every option with its current value in alphabetical order as
.IR name = value ,
one line each. With
.B \-a
or
.BR \-\-all ,
each option with a Bash spelling is followed by a line with its alias and the
same value, posix.legacy.NAME for a POSIX
.B set \-o
option and bash.legacy.NAME otherwise.
.B load
applies a value of
.B KOSHCONF
to the current session.
.B save
prints the current settings, which include the mood and the interactive
options, as the value of
.BR $KOSHCONF .
A kosh started with
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
adopts them. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
.B save
writes the user configuration file instead, holding the mood when it differs
from kosh and every setting that differs from the default of the current mood.
A restricted shell refuses
.B save \-\-persist
and allows a plain
.BR save .
.IP
A boolean is written as on or off and also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0.
editor.history.max_entries is a decimal integer from 0 to 2147483647, and
editor.wrap_width one from 0 to 65535. An unknown option or value, or a file
that cannot be read or written, is an error
with status 1, and a malformed form exits with status 2. An interactive shell
reports on standard error the file written by
.BR create ,
.BR "set \-\-persist" ,
or
.BR "save \-\-persist" .
Only
.B set
and
.B save
accept
.BR \-\-persist ,
and only
.B create
accepts
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.BI "slurp " FILE
FILE is edited in the line editor of the interactive shell, and a missing FILE
starts empty. The session runs on the alternate screen of the terminal and
leaves it before any result is reported, so the main screen keeps only the
command. A shell script, recognized by its shebang or its extension, keeps its
syntax highlighting. Enter and Shift\-Enter start a new line, and in vi normal
mode Enter moves down a line. Tab inserts a tab when only blanks come before
the caret on its line or the file is not a shell script, and completes
otherwise. A tab is drawn as four blank columns on a shaded background, and the
blanks that end a line are shaded too. Each empty line after the last text of
the file has its first column shaded. Ctrl\-X s and Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S in emacs
mode and
.BR :w " and " :write
in vi mode save the file and keep the session open, and the empty status row
reports the save. The vi commands
.BR :wq ", " :wq! ", " :wqa ", " :wqa! ", and " :x
save the file and leave when the save succeeds. The commands
.BR :q " and " :q! ,
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C, and Ctrl\-D on an empty buffer leave without saving, and
Ctrl\-C leaves with status 130. While the buffer differs from the file,
.BR :q " and " :quit
refuse to leave, the other keys and M\-x kill\-kosh leave only when pressed
twice in a row, and
.B :q!
leaves at once. Every colon and M\-x command can be used, and the format
commands change only a shell script. Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R in emacs mode and
.BR / " and " ?
in vi mode find text in the file as it is typed. The
.BR \-\-mode " vi or " "\-\-mode emacs"
option selects the key set for the session, and Up and Down do not recall
shell history.
.IP
The top row repeats the prompt and the command. The file fills every row under
it but the last four, and a file taller than that is shown around the caret.
The last four rows describe the kind of file, its line endings, and its size,
then give the caret line and column with the edit mode, and after an empty row
they list the keys that open the key menu and the commands. Ctrl\-X i in emacs
mode shows the hint of the word under the caret on those rows until the next
key, and Ctrl\-I in vi insert mode does the same when the terminal reports
Ctrl\-I apart from TAB. The M\-x and colon command lines open on the same rows
and leave the view as it was. A mouse click on the file moves the caret there,
and the wheel scrolls the view by three lines, moving the caret only when it
would leave the view. The completion menu opens over the lines under the caret,
or over the lines above it when few rows remain below, and those lines return
when it closes.
.IP
An existing file is saved by writing a complete copy in its directory and
renaming it over the file, which keeps the file mode and follows a symbolic
link to its target. A save that cannot finish is reported, and the file is left
as it was. A file that another user owns, a file with more than one link, a
file in a directory that neither the saving user nor root owns, and a file in a
directory that other users can write to without the sticky bit are
rewritten in place instead, which keeps their owner, group, and links. Before
such a save, the current contents are copied to a private file in the home
directory, or in the temporary directory when the home directory takes none.
The copy is removed once the write succeeds. When the write fails, the
error gives the path of the copy, and when no copy can be made, nothing is
written. The save is refused when another file has replaced the one that slurp
loaded. A refused or failed save is reported on the last four rows, and the
session stays open with the edits. An empty operand, a directory, a special
file, an unreadable file, a file with a NUL byte or invalid UTF\-8, a file
longer than 65534 bytes, a restricted shell, and a session whose standard input
or output is not a terminal are refused with status 1. A wrong operand count or
an unknown mode exits with status 2. A file whose every line ends in CRLF keeps
those endings, a file that mixes them keeps each carriage return as text, and
the final newline of the file is kept.
.TP
.B z
The current directory is changed to one selected from the directories that
interactive shells have visited. A single operand
that is an existing directory is selected as it is. Otherwise the operands form
one case-insensitive substring query, and visit count and recency rank the
matches. With no operand, the home directory is selected.
.SS Compatibility forms
.TP
.B dirs
With no operand, the builtin prints the current directory followed by the saved
stack. A +N operand prints the Nth entry from the top, and a \-N operand prints
the Nth entry from the bottom.
.TP
.BR "pushd \-n DIR" ", " "popd \-n"
The first form adds DIR below the current directory, and the second removes
the entry below it. Neither changes the current directory.
.TP
.B set
With no option and no operand, the shell variables are printed as sorted
assignments. A minus enables an option, and a plus disables it. The forms
.BR "set \-o NAME" " and " "set +o NAME"
select the option by its Bash spelling, and only Bash spellings are accepted. The
OPTION REGISTRY section lists them. With no NAME,
.B set \-o
prints current states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands. A long option such as
.B \-\-mood
is rejected, and the message refers to
.BR koshconf .
The
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-B
forms control errtrace, history expansion, physical directory resolution,
functrace, and brace expansion. The
.B \-N
form controls the exit code diagnostic enabled at startup by
.BR \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes .
The
.B \-t
form and the
.B onecmd
option stop input after one top-level command. The Koshka forms
.BI "set \-M " MOOD
and
.BI "set \-L " MOOD,...
select the mood and source the startup files of other moods as the
command-line options do. Without a value,
.B set \-M
prints the active mood name and
.B set \-L
prints the initialized moods. The
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-G
letters set interpreter.mimic_shebang, debug.print_syntax_tree,
debug.print_lexed_word_escapes, debug.report_every_exit_code,
debug.print_evaluation_statistics, and
debug.print_memory_report_at_exit. Every other Koshka setting belongs to
.BR koshconf .
The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-m
letters are accepted without effect. The
.B \-k
form places unquoted assignment arguments in the command environment. The
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
forms set optimizer.warning_level.
.PP
The
.B history
option records submitted interactive commands, and
.B histexpand
expands interactive history references in the forms
.BR !! ,
.BR !\-N ,
.BR !N ,
.BR !prefix ,
.BR !?text? ,
and
.BR !# .
An event reference accepts the word designators
.BR :^ ,
.BR :$ ,
.BR :* ,
.BR :N ,
.BR :N\-M ,
.BR :\-M ,
.BR :N* ,
.BR :N\-$ ,
and
.BR :N\- ,
and the pathname modifiers
.BR :h ,
.BR :t ,
.BR :r ,
and
.BR :e .
The
.BI :s/ old / new /
modifier replaces the first literal match,
.B :gs
and
.B :as
replace every match, and
.B :Gs
replaces the first match in each word. The
.B :&
modifier repeats the previous substitution. The
.B :q
modifier quotes the selected text as one shell argument, and
.B :x
quotes each word separately. The
.B :p
modifier prints and records the expanded input without executing it. A leading
.BI ^ old ^ new ^
performs the first substitution on the previous event.
.PP
Single-quoted and backslash-escaped references remain literal. When an event,
word, modifier, or substitution error occurs, the command will not execute or
be recorded in history. Both history options are enabled by default in an
interactive shell and disabled in a noninteractive shell.
.PP
Each shell loads one private history branch at startup. An interactive shell
appends submitted commands to the shared file immediately without importing
commands written by other running shells. A noninteractive shell never writes
the file on its own, and only
.B "history \-w"
and
.B "history \-a"
reach the file. KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE limits retained and stored history and
defaults to 4096. A
value of zero disables history retention and storage.
.PP
The
.B ignoreeof
option requires consecutive end-of-file input before an interactive shell
exits. The threshold is the value of
.BR IGNOREEOF .
When IGNOREEOF is unset, enabling the option sets it to 10. Assigning IGNOREEOF
enables the option, and unsetting it disables the option. The
.B nolog
option is accepted for Bash compatibility and does not change execution.
.TP
.B "time [\-p|\-\-posix] [\-R] command [argument ...]"
The command or pipeline is timed. The
.B \-p
and
.B \-\-posix
options select the POSIX report. The default mood adds a processor busy row,
and the peak resident set size when the platform supplies it. The peak is the
highest among the processes that the timed command started and their
descendants. The
.B \-R
option always adds the resident set row. Under the
.B sh
mood time is not a keyword and is resolved on the command search path.
.TP
.B "enable [\-a] [\-d] [\-n] [\-p] [\-s] [\-f filename] [name ...]"
Each operand is enabled, or disabled with
.BR \-n .
An operand can be a builtin or a bundled utility. Each builtin word is a
separate entry, and disabling
.B test
leaves
.B [
enabled. Command lookup skips a disabled builtin and searches PATH and the
bundled utilities. A disabled bundled utility never resolves as a command. The
.B koshkit
builtin still runs a disabled utility. A subshell inherits the state, and the
changes made in a subshell do not reach its parent.
.IP
Without an operand, the enabled builtins and the reachable bundled utilities
are listed as
.B enable
commands. The
.B \-n
option lists the disabled ones as
.B "enable \-n"
commands, and
.B \-a
lists both groups. The
.B \-s
option limits a listing to the special builtins. A builtin that the mood hides,
such as
.B let
in the sh mood, is neither listed nor accepted as an operand. A disabled special
builtin loses its special properties. Koshka has no loadable builtins, so
.B \-f
with an operand fails, and
.B \-d
fails for each operand.
.TP
.B hash
Resolved command locations are remembered or listed. The
.B \-r
option clears the cache. The
.BI "\-p " path
option records path as the location of each command, and a restricted shell
rejects it. The
.B \-t
option prints the remembered location of each command and fails for a command
with no remembered location.
.TP
.B "history [\-cnrwaps] [\-d selector] [operand]"
.br
.B "history {\-S|\-\-sync}"
Without a maintenance option the stored events are listed, and a numeric
operand limits the listing to that many newest events. The
.B \-r
option loads the history file into the list, and an operand gives a file whose
events join the list. The
.B \-n
option loads only the events not yet loaded. The
.B \-w
option writes the list to the history file, and an operand gives a file to
replace. The
.B \-a
option appends the events of the private branch missing from the history file,
or from the file given as an operand. The
.B \-S
and
.B \-\-sync
forms replace the private branch with a snapshot of the shared history file.
The
.B \-c
option clears the private branch and leaves the history file intact, as bash
does, and
.B \-p
prints its operands without storing them. The
.B \-d
option deletes an event number, a negative offset from the newest event, or an
inclusive event range. In an interactive shell, a deleted event will also be
removed from the history file. The
.B \-s
option joins its operands with spaces and stores the result as an event.
.TP
.B "fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]"
The selected history events are edited and executed in the current shell. A
decimal selector gives an event number, a negative selector is relative to
the current history position, and a text selector finds the newest event with
that prefix. Without selectors, editing selects the previous event. The
.B \-l
option lists events without executing them, up to the previous sixteen when no
selectors are given. The
.B \-n
option omits event numbers, and the
.B \-r
option reverses the selected order.
.PP
The editor is selected by
.B \-e
and then by FCEDIT and EDITOR. The default is vi, or ed in POSIX mood. A dash
as the editor skips editing and executes the selected event.
.PP
The form
.B "fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]"
selects one event, applies each literal replacement in order, prints the result
to standard error, and executes it. The return status is the status of that
command.
.TP
.B bind
Flags and operands are accepted without changing key bindings. The builtin
returns zero.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand, the builtin prints nothing and returns one at top level and
zero inside a function or sourced file. A numeric operand selects a call frame
and prints its line number and source file.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern, builtins are printed one per line. A builtin operand
renders the same help as its
.B \-\-help
flag. The
.B \-d
option prints the name and short description, the
.B \-s
option only the usage synopsis, and the
.B \-m
option a manpage-style page.
.TP
.B logout
In a login shell, the builtin runs the EXIT trap and exits with the optional
operand as the status. In a non-login shell, it reports an error and returns
one.
.TP
.B suspend
The shell stops on SIGSTOP and resumes on SIGCONT. A login shell rejects the
operation unless
.B \-f
is present.
.TP
.BR "declare, typeset"
An indexed array is declared by
.BR \-a ,
an associative array by
.BR \-A ,
an arithmetic integer by
.BR \-i ,
lowercase conversion by
.BR \-l ,
read-only assignment by
.BR \-r ,
uppercase conversion by
.BR \-u ,
and an exported variable by
.BR \-x .
The plus forms remove the integer and case-conversion attributes.
The
.B \-f
and
.B \-F
options restrict the operands to functions, printing each recorded definition or
only its name. The
.B \-p
option prints the matching declarations. Without operands, the
.B \-p
option or an attribute option lists every variable carrying the requested
attributes. The
.B \-n
option makes the variable a reference to the variable selected by its value, which
may carry an array subscript. An expansion, an assignment, an append, an
export, and an unset of the reference act on that variable, and a chain of
references is followed. ${!name} gives the name reached by the reference, and
.B "unset \-n"
removes the reference itself. The conditional [[ \-R name ]] is true for a
reference with a value. A reference with no value binds to the variable given by
its first assignment. A reference to itself is an error at the top level, and a
circular chain counts as unset with a warning and fails an assignment. A write
through a reference applies the read-only, restricted, integer, and case rules
of the target. A read-only variable, PATH, IFS, and a dynamic variable other than
RANDOM and SECONDS cannot become a reference. The
.B +n
form removes the reference and keeps its value. The
.B \-g
and
.B \-t
options are accepted without effect.
.TP
.B local
The variable is local to the running function. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.B \-x
attributes act as for declare, and
.B "local \-n"
passes a caller's variable by reference. A
.B \-
operand saves the
.B set
options, and the function return restores them.
.TP
.B readonly
Each given variable is marked read-only. The
.B \-p
option lists the read-only variables in a reusable form. The
.B \-a
and
.B \-A
options treat each operand as an indexed or associative array. The
.B \-f
option marks each given function read-only, and it cannot be redefined or
unset. A bare
.B \-f
lists each read-only function with its body.
.TP
.BR "mapfile, readarray"
The
.B \-t
option stores input lines in an array without their delimiters. The
.B \-n
option limits the line count, and
.B \-s
discards leading lines. The
.B \-O
option selects the first array index. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter with the first byte of its value. The
.B \-u
option takes input from a file descriptor. The
.B \-C
option evaluates its callback with the destination index and current record as
arguments after each quantum of records, and
.B \-c
sets the quantum, 5000 records by default.
.TP
.B printf
The
.B \-v
option assigns the formatted result to a variable. In every mood, the numeric
locale is taken from the first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC, and LANG
as the shell holds them at each call. Floating operands are parsed and printed
with the decimal point of that locale. The
.B \(aq
flag groups the digits of integer and floating conversions with the thousands
separator of the locale. The C and POSIX locales and every locale on Windows
use a dot and no grouping. Arithmetic expansion does not use the locale.
.TP
.B read
The
.B \-r
option retains backslashes, and a plain read treats a trailing backslash as a
line continuation. The
.B \-a
option splits the line into the indexed array NAME. The
.B \-d
option replaces the newline delimiter, and an empty value selects a NUL byte.
The
.B \-n
option sets the maximum byte count. The
.B \-N
option takes exactly that many bytes, ignores the delimiter, and stores them
unsplit in the first variable. The
.B \-t
option limits the wait in seconds. The
.B \-p
option prints a prompt at a terminal, and
.B \-s
keeps terminal input unechoed. The
.B \-u
option takes input from a file descriptor. The
.B \-q
option takes one byte and returns zero for y or Y. The
.B \-e
option is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B compgen
The
.BI "\-V " varname
option stores the candidates in the given indexed array and writes nothing to
standard output. A generator matching no candidates leaves the array empty and
returns one. The
.BI "\-P " prefix
and
.BI "\-S " suffix
options add text to each candidate after word prefix matching and
.B \-X
filtering.
.TP
.B complete
A registered spec offers the candidates of its actions, its
.B \-G
glob, its
.B \-W
word list, and, on an explicit Tab, the COMPREPLY of its
.B \-F
function and the output lines of its
.B \-C
command, in that order. The
.B \-C
command runs as a command substitution with the command name, the word, and
the previous word appended as quoted arguments, and with COMP_LINE,
COMP_POINT, COMP_KEY, and COMP_TYPE exported. Each nonempty line of its output
is a candidate. The ghost suggestion never runs a function or a command and
offers only actions answered from memory. The actions
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.BI "\-A " action
list what
.B compgen
lists for the same action, and the glob keeps the matches starting with the
word. The
.B \-X
filter then removes candidates by the rules of
.BR "compgen \-X" ,
and
.B \-P
and
.B \-S
add their text to each remaining candidate. The
.B \-o dirnames
option adds the directories starting with the word when nothing else
matched, and
.B \-o plusdirs
adds them always. As in Bash, a spec quotes the special characters of inserted
candidates only when it generates filenames.
.B \-o fullquote
quotes every candidate, and
.B \-o noquote
leaves filenames unquoted.
.B \-o nosort
keeps the candidates in the order the spec generated them.
.B \-o nospace
inserts no space after an accepted candidate.
An empty result falls through to the next completion source. An unknown
.B \-o
option or
.B \-A
action is an error with status 2, and nothing is registered.
.IP
With
.B progcomp_alias
on, a command with no spec of its own uses the spec of the first word of its
alias. An explicit Tab also follows a command found through PATH to the name
of its symlink target. A command with no spec reached this way uses the
.B \-D
default spec. When its function returns 124, completion restarts with the spec
the function registered for the command. The
.B \-E
spec serves an explicit Tab on an empty line, and the
.B \-I
spec serves the command word. Their functions and commands receive
.B _EmptycmD_
and
.B _InitialWorD_
as the command name. Given more than one of
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.BR \-I ,
the first in that order is used and command words are ignored.
.IP
The
.B \-p
option prints each spec in the order Bash prints them. The
.B \-r
option removes the specified specs or the given slot, and every spec when given
neither. A name or slot with no spec is an error.
.TP
.B compopt
The
.BI "\-o " option
form turns a
.B complete \-o
option on and
.BI "+o " option
turns it off, and off wins when both refer to the same option. The change
applies to the spec of each given command, or to the
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
or
.B \-I
spec, the first in that order, in place of the operands. Inside a running
completion function with neither, it applies to the current completion only.
Without
.B \-o
or
.BR +o ,
the options are printed as one
.B compopt
line for each spec. An unknown option name is an error with status 2 that
changes nothing. A name or slot with no spec is an error with status 1.
Outside a completion function, a call with no name and no slot is an error with
status 1.
.TP
.B type
The
.B \-V
option prints everything the shell holds about each name, such as the file and
line where a function is defined, followed by its body, or the description of
a builtin or bundled utility. The
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-P
options keep their terse output and ignore
.BR \-V .
Under the bash moods a function is reported as "is a function" and its body is
printed. Every other mood reports "is a shell function" and prints nothing
after it.
.SH KOSHKIT UTILITIES
The
.B koshkit
builtin runs a bundled utility. An unknown utility is an error. Every utility
supports \fB\-\-help\fR, and one invoked through a matching executable name also
supports \fB\-\-version\fR. The
.B koshkit \-\-list
form prints the utilities one per line.
.PP
With no utility,
.B koshkit
groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings.
.PP
The bundled set contains basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown,
cksum, cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env, evil,
evildisk, evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet, evilps, evilss,
expand, expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf, goodcore, goodfsw,
goodnode, goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, link, ln, locale, logger,
logname, ls, make,
man, meow, mkdir, mkfifo, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste, pathchk,
pkill, pr, printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir, sed, seq,
sleep, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee, timeout, touch,
tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink, watch, wc, which, who,
whoami, xargs, and yes.
.PP
The command
.BI "koshkit \-\-assimilate " DIR
installs one symlink per utility into DIR. When direct koshkit lookup is
enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a bundled utility. A shell
started under a utility name runs that utility. Started as
.BR koshkit ,
the link that
.B make install
places next to
.BR kosh ,
it runs the utility given by its first operand, such as
.BR "koshkit ls \-l" ,
and otherwise acts as the koshkit builtin. Either form skips the shell
startup, loading no settings or startup file and ignoring KOSH_FLAGS.
.TP
.B basename
The final path component is printed, minus an optional suffix operand. The
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-multiple\fR option treats every operand as a path, and
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-suffix\fR removes its suffix from every path and implies
\fB\-a\fR. The \fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\fR option ends each entry with a NUL
byte.
.TP
.B cal
A Gregorian month or year is printed, each week starting on Sunday. The
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-today\fR
option starts each week on Monday and prints the current date in words below
the calendar.
.TP
.B calc
Arithmetic operands are evaluated. The \fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR and
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pipe\fR options take expressions interactively or from a pipe.
Arbitrary precision integers and finite decimal values are accepted in every
mood. The abs, atan, ceil, cmp, cos, exp, fact, fib, floor, frac, gcd, int,
iseven, isint, isodd, length, lcm, ln, max, min, scale, sgn, sin, and sqrt
functions are available. Ctrl-R in the interactive prompt searches the calc
history.
.TP
.B bc
Expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow, input and output
bases, and scale are supported. The \fB\-l\fR option enables atan, cos, exp, ln,
and sin and selects a scale of 20.
.TP
.B cat
Files or standard input are concatenated, with line numbering (\fB\-n\fR)
continuing across files. The \fB\-b\fR option numbers only nonempty lines, and
\fB\-s\fR writes one empty line for each run of empty lines. The \fB\-v\fR
option writes control bytes in ^ notation and bytes above 127 with an M\-
prefix, except tab and newline. The \fB\-E\fR option writes $ at the end of
each line, and a carriage return before a newline as ^M. The \fB\-T\fR option
writes a tab as ^I. The \fB\-e\fR, \fB\-t\fR, and \fB\-A\fR options act as
\fB\-vE\fR, \fB\-vT\fR, and \fB\-vET\fR. The \fB\-u\fR option is accepted. The
\fB\-\-syntax\-highlighting\fR
option colors recognized shell source when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.BR chgrp ", " chown
The group, or the owner and optional group, of each file is changed. The
\fB\-R\fR option recurses, \fB\-H\fR, \fB\-L\fR, and \fB\-P\fR select how
symbolic links are followed, and \fB\-h\fR changes a link instead of its target.
The \fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR
option keeps recursion on the file system of each operand.
.TP
.B chmod
Symbolic or octal modes are applied to each file. The
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR
option changes directories and their contents without following symbolic
links. The \fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR option skips directories on a file system
other than that of the operand. It has no short form.
.TP
.B comm
Two sorted files are merged into columns of lines only in the first file, only
in the second file, and in both. The \fB\-1\fR, \fB\-2\fR, and \fB\-3\fR
options suppress those columns. A line out of order after an unpaired line is
reported once per file, and the status is 1. The \fB\-\-check\-order\fR
option stops at the first line out of order even when every line pairs, and
\fB\-\-nocheck\-order\fR skips the check; the later of the two applies. The
\fB\-\-output\-delimiter\fR option separates columns with its value instead of
a tab, or with NUL when the value is empty. The \fB\-\-total\fR option ends
the output with the count of each column and the word total. The \fB\-z\fR
option separates input and output lines with NUL.
.TP
.B cp
Files and directory trees are copied. The \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-R\fR, \fB\-f\fR,
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-p\fR, and \fB\-v\fR options are supported. A recursive copy
keeps symbolic links as links. The \fB\-L\fR option follows every symbolic link,
\fB\-H\fR follows only a link given on the command line, and \fB\-P\fR copies a
link as a link. A symbolic link leading back into a directory being copied is
reported as cyclic and skipped, and the status is 1. The
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR
option copies a directory on another file system as an empty directory.
.TP
.B df
Mounted filesystems or the filesystems of the operands are reported. The
\fB\-k\fR and \fB\-P\fR options select 1024-byte blocks and the POSIX header.
The \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-human\-readable\fR
option prints sizes in powers of 1024, and the \fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-si\fR
option does the same in powers of 1000. The rows form a table whose figures are
right-aligned, and filesystems that cannot be read are reported before it.
.TP
.B diff
Two files are compared line by line. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors the output. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option
means auto. Without the option, the color mode of koshkit decides. The
\fB\-u\fR,
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-a\fR, and repeated \fB\-L\fR
options select unified output, ignore whitespace, force text input, and set up
to two unified header labels. Equal files return zero, different files return
one, and usage or input errors return two.
.TP
.B dirname
The directory portion of each path is printed. The \fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\fR
option ends each entry with a NUL byte.
.TP
.B du
Every file and directory below each path is printed in descending size order,
without following symbolic links. With no path, the current directory is used.
The \fB\-s\fR option prints only each path total,
and \fB\-h\fR prints human-readable sizes. The \fB\-x\fR,
\fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR
option skips every entry on a file system other than that of its path operand.
The \fB\-\-tree\fR option prints the same entries as a tree. The
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-top\-largest\fR
option prints a human-readable tree of only the largest entries and their
ancestors. A file or directory that cannot be read because permission is denied
is reported as a warning, and other read failures are errors. Either kind makes
the status 1.
.TP
.B env
Temporary assignments are applied before a command, or the environment is
printed. The \fB\-i\fR option starts with an empty environment, and each
\fB\-u\fR option removes one variable. The command is found through its
.BR PATH ,
or through /usr/bin:/bin when it has none.
.TP
.BR expand ", " unexpand
Tabs are converted to spaces, or runs of blanks to tabs. The \fB\-t\fR option
takes ascending tab stops separated by commas or blanks. A single value sets a
tab every that many columns. A last value written /N adds a stop at every
multiple of N after the last explicit stop, and +N adds a stop every N columns
after it. Without \fB\-t\fR, a tab stop falls every eight columns. The
\fB\-a\fR option of unexpand converts blanks after the first nonblank
character, and \fB\-t\fR implies it.
.TP
.B xargs
Arguments read from standard input are appended to a command. The
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-arg\-file\fR
option reads the arguments from a file instead, and the command keeps standard
input. The \fB\-0\fR option splits the input on null bytes, and
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-delimiter\fR
splits it on one character, which can be an escape such as \en, \et, \ex41,
or \e0. Both keep quotes and backslashes literal and disable the end-of-input
marker. The \fB\-r\fR option skips the command when the input is empty. The
\fB\-E\fR option sets the end-of-input marker, and
\fB\-e\fR[\fIEOF\fR], \fB\-\-eof\fR[=\fIEOF\fR]
sets it from an attached value or removes it. The \fB\-I\fR option replaces a
string in the command with each input line, and
\fB\-i\fR[\fIR\fR], \fB\-\-replace\fR[=\fIR\fR]
does the same with an attached string or {}. The \fB\-L\fR, \fB\-n\fR, and
\fB\-s\fR options limit each command to a number of lines, arguments, or bytes.
The \fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-max\-procs\fR
option runs up to that many external commands at once, or as many as possible
for 0, and their output can interleave. The \fB\-p\fR option asks before each
command runs, \fB\-t\fR traces each command, and \fB\-x\fR fails when a
command does not fit in the size limit. The status is 123 when a command
fails, 124 when one exits with 255, 125 when a signal ends one, and 126 or
127 when one cannot run. With \fB\-P\fR, a command that cannot run stops the
run after the running commands finish.
.TP
.B od
Input bytes are written in the formats selected by \fB\-t\fR. The \fB\-b\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-o\fR, \fB\-s\fR, and \fB\-x\fR options select the o1,
c, u2, o2, d2, and x2 formats.
Each output line holds 16 bytes. The
.BR \-w [ \fIcount\fR ]
or
.BR \-\-width [= \fIcount\fR ]
option writes
.I count
bytes per line, or 32 without a count. A count that is not a multiple of the
largest selected unit size is replaced by that size with a warning.
.TP
.B paste
Corresponding lines of the files are joined with tabs. The \fB\-d\fR option
supplies delimiters that are used in turn, and \fB\-s\fR joins the lines of
each file into one line. The \fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\-terminated\fR option
ends input and output lines with a null byte.
.TP
.B pathchk
Each pathname is checked against the component and pathname limits of the file
system. The \fB\-p\fR option uses the POSIX portable limits and filename
characters, and \fB\-P\fR rejects an empty pathname and a component that begins
with a hyphen. The \fB\-W\fR or \fB\-\-windows\fR option rejects components that
Windows cannot hold, such as reserved devices. Windows builds always apply
these rules.
.TP
.B printenv
The whole environment is printed when no name is given. The status is one if
any name is absent.
.TP
.B file
Filesystem object kinds and file contents are classified. The \fB\-h\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-dereference\fR
option classifies a symbolic link, and \fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-dereference\fR follows
it. The \fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-regular\-only\fR option skips content tests. The
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-magic\-file\fR option adds a magic database, and
\fB\-M\fR, \fB\-\-magic\-only\fR uses only the given databases.
.TP
.B find
Sorted trees are walked with \fB\-name\fR, \fB\-iname\fR, \fB\-type\fR,
\fB\-maxdepth\fR, \fB\-mindepth\fR, \fB\-print\fR, and \fB\-print0\fR. The
\fB\-xdev\fR and \fB\-mount\fR
primaries keep the walk on the file system of its starting point. The
.B \-exec
action runs a command ending in a
.B ;
argument for each matched path, replacing each
.B {}
with the path. A command ending in
.B +
after
.B {}
receives the matched paths in batches.
.TP
.B flock
A command runs while a directory lock is held. The
.B \-\-transaction\-held\-lock
option keeps the lock after the process that launched the transaction exits.
.TP
.B fuser
Process IDs that use each given file are printed. The \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-f\fR, and
\fB\-u\fR options are supported.
.TP
.B getconf
System and string variables take one name. Path variables also take a path.
An undefined or unsupported variable prints
.BR undefined .
The \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
option prints every known variable as a name and value. The
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-specification\fR
option selects a supported POSIX environment.
.TP
.B grep
Regular-expression matches are printed. The
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
option colors matches. WHEN is auto, always, or never, and a bare option means
auto. Without the option, the color mode of koshkit applies. When color is on,
GREP_COLORS holds colon-separated GNU capabilities such as
.BR ms=01;31 .
The capabilities ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv take effect, an unknown
capability is ignored, and an empty value turns that color off. The deprecated
GREP_COLOR sets the match color at a lower precedence than GREP_COLORS.
.PP
The \fB\-i\fR, \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-n\fR, and \fB\-h\fR
options ignore case, invert matches, recurse into directories, show line
numbers, and suppress filename prefixes. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option makes \fB\-r\fR
skip every directory on a file system other than that of its operand. It has
no short form. Options may follow the
operands.
A pattern is a basic regular expression. The \fB\-E\fR
option treats patterns as extended regular expressions and \fB\-F\fR
treats them as fixed strings. The two options conflict and exit with
status 2. Each \fB\-e\fR option adds a pattern, and each \fB\-f\fR option adds
the lines of a file as patterns. When either option is given, every
operand is a file. A newline inside a pattern separates two patterns. The
\fB\-x\fR
option selects only lines that match entirely. The \fB\-c\fR
option prints the count of selected lines for each file. The \fB\-l\fR
option prints each file with a selected line once, and takes precedence over
\fB\-c\fR. The \fB\-q\fR
option prints nothing and exits with status 0 at the first selected line. The
\fB\-s\fR
option suppresses messages about unreadable files. A file with a NUL byte is
a binary file: matching lines in the 96 KiB blocks before the block that
holds its first NUL byte print, and a later match prints
.B "grep: FILE: binary file matches"
once instead of its lines. The
.BR \-a " (" \-\-text )
option reads such a file as text. The exit status is 0 when a
line is selected, 1 when no line is selected, and 2 on an error. In a UTF-8
locale, a dot and a bracket expression match one whole character. In every
other locale, they match one byte.
.TP
.B head
The first ten lines are printed by default. The \fB\-n\fR
option selects a line count and \fB\-c\fR
a byte count. A count with a leading dash prints all but that many trailing
lines or bytes. A count may end in b for 512, in k, K, m, M, G, T, P, E, Z,
Y, R, or Q for a power of 1024, or in one of those letters followed by B for a
power of 1000, such as \fB1kB\fR, or by iB, such as \fB1KiB\fR. A first
argument of a dash and digits, such as \fB\-5\fR, is the same as
\fB\-n 5\fR. Filename headers are printed for several files. The \fB\-q\fR,
\fB\-\-quiet\fR, and \fB\-\-silent\fR options never print them, and \fB\-v\fR,
\fB\-\-verbose\fR always print them. The last of these options takes effect.
The \fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\-terminated\fR option ends lines with a null
byte.
.TP
.B tail
The last ten lines are printed by default, with \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-c\fR, size
suffixes, and the dash-and-digits first argument as for head. A count with a
leading plus, such as \fB+5\fR, starts at that line or byte. The \fB\-q\fR,
\fB\-\-quiet\fR, and \fB\-\-silent\fR
options never print filename headers, and \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR always
print them. The last of these options takes effect. The \fB\-z\fR,
\fB\-\-zero\-terminated\fR option ends lines with a null byte. The \fB\-f\fR
option keeps each regular file open after the initial output and prints the
bytes appended to it. The \fB\-F\fR
option follows the file by name, reopens a file that is replaced, removed, or
created later, and implies \fB\-\-retry\fR. The
.BI \-\-follow= descriptor|name
option selects the mode. The
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-sleep\-interval\fR
option sets the longest wait between checks in seconds, one
second by default. The
.BI \-\-pid= pid
option ends the follow after that process exits. An interrupted follow ends
with status 130.
.TP
.B killall
Processes are signaled by exact name. The signal is selected and the signals
are listed with \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-signal\fR, \fB\-l\fR, and
\fB\-\-list\fR.
.TP
.B pkill
Processes whose short command (comm) contains the pattern are signaled, with
the signal selected and listed as for killall.
.TP
.B ln
Hard links are created by default. The \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-L\fR, and
\fB\-P\fR
options create a symbolic link, remove an existing destination first, follow a
symbolic link source, and link a symbolic link source itself.
.TP
.B logname
The login session user name is printed. It can differ from the effective user
reported by
.BR whoami .
.TP
.B ls
Sorted entries are listed with \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-A\fR, \fB\-1\fR, \fB\-l\fR, and
\fB\-h\fR. The \fB\-F\fR
option appends a type indicator to each name, and \fB\-p\fR
appends a slash to each directory. The \fB\-d\fR
option lists a directory operand as itself. The \fB\-n\fR
option prints numeric owner and group identifiers, and \fB\-g\fR and \fB\-o\fR
print the long form without the owner or without the group. The \fB\-k\fR
option is accepted. The \fB\-t\fR, \fB\-S\fR,
and \fB\-r\fR
options sort by modification time, by size, and in reverse. The \fB\-R\fR
option lists every subdirectory that is reached, and \fB\-\-tree\fR
prints an indented directory tree. The \fB\-L\fR
option sets the maximum recursion depth. The
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
option keeps \fB\-R\fR and \fB\-\-tree\fR
on the file system of the operand. It has no short form. The \fB\-\-color\fR
option accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B make
Compact make rules are run. The \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR, \fB\-C\fR, and
\fB\-\-directory\fR
options are supported. The \fB\-B\fR
option always rebuilds targets, and \fB\-k\fR
continues independent goals after an error. The \fB\-e\fR, \fB\-i\fR,
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-p\fR, \fB\-q\fR, \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-s\fR, and \fB\-t\fR
options provide the POSIX make execution modes. The \fB\-j\fR
extension takes an optional decimal job count, propagated through MAKEFLAGS.
Common variable functions include call, eval, filter, filter-out, foreach,
patsubst, shell, sort, and wildcard. The wildcard function and a
prerequisite that holds *, ?, or [ expand through the pathname expansion of the
shell, and a prerequisite pattern without a match stays as written.
.TP
.B meow
A screensaver of pixel-art cats is shown. The
\fB\-f\fR or \fB\-\-fps\fR
option sets the frames drawn per second, from 1 to 240, with 60 by default. The
\fB\-s\fR or \fB\-\-speed\fR
option runs the scene faster or slower, from 0.1 to 10, and the
\fB\-n\fR or \fB\-\-cats\fR
option shows from 1 to 8 cats. Space pauses, \fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR
change the speed, and any other key ends the screensaver.
.TP
.B mkdir
Directories are created with \fB\-p\fR
and an octal or symbolic \fB\-m\fR
mode. A symbolic mode is applied to mode 777 with the chmod grammar.
.TP
.B mknod
FIFO, character, and block special files are created with POSIX type operands
or \fB\-\-type\fR, \fB\-\-fifo\fR, \fB\-\-character\fR,
and \fB\-\-block\fR
options. Device nodes accept \fB\-\-major\fR and \fB\-\-minor\fR
numbers, and the \fB\-m\fR option sets the permission mode.
.TP
.B mv
Sources are renamed with \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR, and \fB\-v\fR.
.TP
.B nl
Input lines are numbered within logical pages. Lines that consist only of the
section delimiter repeated three, two, or one times start a header, body, or
footer section and are written as empty lines. The \fB\-d\fR option sets the
delimiter, which defaults to \e: and gains a colon when one character is given.
An empty delimiter disables sections. The \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-b\fR, and \fB\-f\fR
options select the header, body, and footer style: a numbers every line, t
nonempty lines, n no lines, and p\fIBRE\fR lines that match the basic regular
expression. The defaults are n, t, and n. Each section restarts the numbering
at the \fB\-v\fR value unless \fB\-p\fR is given. The \fB\-i\fR option sets the
increment, and both values can be negative. The \fB\-l\fR option numbers only
every given empty line of a run under the a style. The \fB\-w\fR,
\fB\-n\fR, and \fB\-s\fR options set the number width, the ln, rn, or rz
format, and the separator, which can be empty. A line number that overflows
stops the run with status 1 before the next numbered line.
.TP
.B nproc
The available logical processor count is printed. The \fB\-\-all\fR and
\fB\-\-ignore=COUNT\fR options are supported.
.TP
.B ps
Processes are listed with \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-u\fR, and \fB\-x\fR. The \fB\-w\fR
option is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B readlink
The target of each symbolic link is printed on its own line. A file that is not
a link is reported and makes the status 1. The
.B \-n
option suppresses the trailing newline of a single file.
.TP
.B realpath
The canonical absolute form of each path is printed. Every component but the
last must exist, every component must exist with \fB\-e\fR, and none needs to
exist with \fB\-m\fR. Symbolic links are resolved as they are encountered, or
with \fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-physical\fR. The \fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-logical\fR
option resolves '..' components before symbolic links, and \fB\-s\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-symlinks\fR, \fB\-\-strip\fR leaves symbolic links unresolved.
The last of these three options applies. The \fB\-\-relative\-to\fR option
prints each path relative to a directory. The \fB\-\-relative\-base\fR option
prints paths below a directory relative to it and other paths absolute. With
both options, paths are printed relative to the first directory when it is
below the base directory, and absolute otherwise. The \fB\-q\fR,
\fB\-\-quiet\fR option omits the error messages for unresolved paths, and
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\fR ends each entry with a NUL byte.
.TP
.B rm
Paths are removed with \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-R\fR, \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR, and
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR.
Root, dot, and dot-dot are refused, including every spelling of a root
directory and a Windows drive root. The
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
option skips a directory on a file system other than that of its path
operand and makes the status 1.
.TP
.B rmdir
Empty directories are removed. The
.B \-p
option also removes empty parent directories.
.TP
.B seq
A sequence of numbers is printed with a nonzero step. Integer operands print
exactly. Decimal, exponent, and hexadecimal operands print with the most
fraction digits of the first operand and the step. The \fB\-w\fR,
\fB\-\-equal\-width\fR option pads every value with leading zeros to one
width. The \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-separator\fR option places a string between
values, and the final value still ends with a newline. The \fB\-f\fR,
\fB\-\-format\fR option formats each value with one printf %e, %f, %g, or
%a conversion and cannot be combined with \fB\-w\fR.
.TP
.B sleep
Execution pauses for summed durations with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.B sort
Input lines are sorted in byte order, or by the keys given with
.BR \-k .
A key is
.IR F[.C][bdfghiMnrV][,F[.C][bdfghiMnrV]] .
The \fB\-t\fR option sets the field separator byte. The \fB\-b\fR, \fB\-d\fR,
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR, \fB\-n\fR, and \fB\-r\fR options ignore leading blanks,
compare only blanks and alphanumeric characters, fold lowercase letters,
compare only printable characters, compare leading numbers by value, and
reverse the result. The \fB\-g\fR option compares leading floating point
numbers, including exponents, infinities, and NaN, and places text without a
number first. The \fB\-h\fR option compares numbers with the K, M, G, T, P,
E, Z, Y, R, and Q suffixes, so 2K sorts before 1M. The \fB\-M\fR option
compares three-letter month abbreviations in any case and places other text
first. The \fB\-V\fR option compares runs of digits by value within text, as
version numbers. At most one of \fB\-g\fR, \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-M\fR, and
\fB\-n\fR applies to a key, and none of them combines with \fB\-d\fR,
\fB\-i\fR, or \fB\-V\fR. The \fB\-z\fR option separates input and output
lines with NUL. The \fB\-u\fR option writes the first of each run of equal
keys. The \fB\-c\fR option checks that the input is sorted and reports the
first line out of order, and \fB\-C\fR checks it without a message. The
\fB\-o\fR option writes the result to a file. The \fB\-s\fR option skips the
last-resort comparison of whole lines, and \fB\-m\fR is accepted for merging
sorted input.
.TP
.B stty
Terminal settings are reported or changed. The \fB\-a\fR option reports all
settings, and \fB\-g\fR reports an encoded state that can be supplied later to
restore the settings.
.TP
.B tee
Standard input is copied to output files. The \fB\-a\fR option appends.
.TP
.B timeout
A command runs with a time limit. The duration accepts a suffix. The
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-signal\fR, \fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kill\-after\fR, \fB\-p\fR, and
\fB\-\-preserve\-status\fR options select the signal, send KILL after a further
duration, and return the command status.
.TP
.B touch
File times are updated. The \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-m\fR, \fB\-r\fR, and
\fB\-t\fR options select the access time, suppress creation of a missing file,
select the modification time, specify a reference file, and set an explicit
timestamp.
.TP
.B cut
Bytes, characters, or fields are selected from each line with \fB\-b\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, or \fB\-f\fR. The \fB\-d\fR option sets the field delimiter, and
\fB\-s\fR skips lines without one. The
.B \-\-complement
option selects the positions outside the list. The
.BI \-\-output\-delimiter= str
option joins selected fields, and separate byte or character ranges, with
.IR str .
The \fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zero\-terminated\fR option ends input and output
lines with a null byte.
.TP
.B tr
Input bytes are translated with ranges, character classes, [=c=] equivalence
classes, and [c*n] repeats. The \fB\-d\fR option deletes bytes, \fB\-c\fR
and \fB\-C\fR replace the first set with every byte outside it, and
\fB\-s\fR squeezes each run of a repeated byte from the last set into one
byte. When the first set is longer, the second set is extended with its last
byte, unless \fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-truncate\-set1\fR limits the translation to
the length of the second set. When translating, the second set accepts only
the [:upper:] and [:lower:] classes, each aligned with an [:upper:] or
[:lower:] class in the first set, and it accepts one [c*] repeat that fills
the second set to the length of the first. A [c*] repeat in the first set is
an error.
.TP
.B uniq
Adjacent equal lines are collapsed. The \fB\-c\fR option prints run counts.
The \fB\-d\fR and \fB\-u\fR options write only lines that have a repeat or only
lines that have none. The \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-s\fR options ignore leading fields
and characters when comparing lines. The \fB\-w\fR option compares at most
the given number of characters after them, and \fB\-i\fR compares ASCII
letters without case. The \fB\-D\fR option writes every line of each run
that has a repeat, and \fB\-\-all\-repeated\fR does the same with an empty
line before each run for prepend or between runs for separate. The
\fB\-\-group\fR option writes every line with an empty line between runs,
before each run for prepend, after each run for append, or both, and cannot
be combined with \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-D\fR, or \fB\-u\fR. The
\fB\-z\fR option separates input and output lines with NUL. A second operand
is the output file.
.TP
.B unlink
One non-directory path is removed.
.TP
.B evil
System identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap, root filesystem, process,
filesystem, and network information is printed in titled sections. The
\fB\-a\fR option adds system details omitted from the default report, \fB\-s\fR
selects identity fields, \fB\-u\fR prints every local user account, and
\fB\-\-color\fR accepts always, auto, and never.
.TP
.B goodcore
A running process can be captured with \fB\-p\fR, and an existing core can be
packaged with its executable through \fB\-b\fR.
.TP
.B eviliso
Isolation information is grouped into namespace, cgroup, login-session, remote
socket, container-runtime, container, and Kubernetes sections. With no
selector, all sections are shown. Selectors combine. The \fB\-n\fR
(\fB\-\-namespaces\fR), \fB\-c\fR (\fB\-\-cgroups\fR), \fB\-s\fR
(\fB\-\-sessions\fR), and \fB\-r\fR (\fB\-\-remote\fR) options select
namespaces, cgroups, sessions, and remote sockets, and \fB\-k\fR
(\fB\-\-runtime\fR) selects container-runtime data. The long-only
\fB\-\-container\fR and \fB\-\-kubernetes\fR selectors select their own
sections, and \fB\-\-containers\fR is an alias for \fB\-\-container\fR. The
\fB\-a\fR or \fB\-\-all\fR option adds detailed rows. The Kubernetes section
takes its values from the
.B KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST
variable, the service-account namespace file, and
.B kubepods
cgroup components.
.TP
.B evildisk
Capacity and filesystem identity are printed for each operand path or
mounted filesystem, along with native disk error and retry totals when the
operating system provides them. With \fB\-a\fR, mount labels, UUIDs,
filesystem error counters, and SMART data are included when the platform
exposes them.
.TP
.B evilfiles
Open files are listed for visible processes. The \fB\-p\fR, \fB\-u\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-i\fR, and \fB\-t\fR options filter by process, owner, command
prefix, socket descriptor, and terse process identifier output. The
\fB\-w\fR and \fB\-\-wide\fR options keep the command and name columns from
being shortened to the terminal width.
.TP
.B evilfs
Mounted filesystem sources, targets, types, and options are printed in a sorted
table.
.TP
.B evilio
Current memory, swap, and cumulative disk I/O counters are printed without
waiting. The \fB\-a\fR option adds processor usage, paging, scheduler state,
pressure stalls, disk activity, and swap rates from a one second sample. The
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-human-readable\fR options select compact binary units for
byte sizes, which is the default. The \fB\-\-ps\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-p\fR, and
\fB\-NUMBER\fR options print per-process I/O counters. They show every
process, limit the table, select one process, or limit the table to NUMBER rows.
.PP
The \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-\-live\fR options print the report repeatedly until
interrupted. Their optional value, N, is the number of seconds between samples
and between refreshes, and defaults to 0.5. Output uses the alternate screen
and restores the previous contents on exit. q quits with status 0, and an
interrupt quits with status 130. A frame taller than the terminal scrolls with
\fBj\fR, \fBk\fR, the arrow keys, the page keys, \fBg\fR, \fBG\fR, and, on a
POSIX terminal, the mouse wheel.
.PP
The \fB\-\-cumulative\fR option waits for M seconds, defaulting to one second,
and prints the counter change over that window. When combined with
\fB\-\-live\fR, it sets the length of a rolling window keeping the counter
changes of the last M seconds, and \fB\-\-live\fR alone sets the sampling and
refresh interval.
.PP
The \fB\-\-sort\fR option orders rows by a full metric name or its shortest
unambiguous prefix. Process reports accept pid, read, write, read-ops, and
write-ops. Disk reports accept read, write, read-ops, write-ops, busy,
read-latency, write-latency, average-queue, queue, errors, and retries. Metrics
sort from largest to smallest, and process identifiers sort from smallest to
largest. Busy, latency, and average queue values require a sampled report.
.TP
.B evillogs
Core dump settings, recognized dump reports, and known log directories are
printed. The \fB\-\-cores\fR and \fB\-\-logs\fR options select one report.
.TP
.B evilnet
Assigned IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are printed in a sorted table. The \fB\-a\fR
option adds interface traffic, packet errors, packet drops, and a TCP counter
table. The \fB\-f\fR option prints TCP failure and packet-loss telemetry
without interface or socket rows. The \fB\-t\fR and \fB\-\-traffic\fR options
print only the interface traffic table. Live and cumulative options behave as
for evilio. The \fB\-\-sort\fR option orders traffic rows by name, receive or
transmit bytes, packet counts, errors, or drops, using the shortest
unambiguous prefix.
.TP
.B goodnode
Inode identity, allocation, known filesystem capabilities, verification state,
and a CRC32C checksum are printed for each path. The \fB\-i\fR option locates
an inode beneath the path selected by \fB\-r\fR. The \fB\-\-verify\fR option
runs the native filesystem verification of the platform when it supports it.
.TP
.B evilps
Visible processes are printed as a parent and child tree. The \fB\-p\fR,
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-A\fR, \fB\-U\fR, \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-M\fR, and
\fB\-w\fR options show process identifiers, sort numerically, enable every
field, show command lines, show owners, show processor usage, show resident
memory, and keep command lines at full length. The \fB\-\-wide\fR option is
equivalent to \fB\-w\fR. The \fB\-\-sort\fR option accepts name, pid, cpu,
memory, or an unambiguous prefix of one of those. The \fB\-NUMBER\fR operand
limits the output to NUMBER processes. The \fB\-C\fR option is shorthand for
\fB\-\-cumulative\fR.
.PP
The \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-\-live\fR options sample and refresh the process tree
until interrupted, with N as for evilio. On a terminal, the scroll keys of
evilio apply, \fBs\fR cycles the sort key, and \fB/\fR starts a search, applied
with Enter and cleared with Escape. The \fB\-\-cumulative\fR option sets a
window of M seconds, defaulting to one second. Without \fB\-\-live\fR, two
snapshots are taken M seconds apart and the report shows the average
processor utilization between them.
.TP
.B evilss
Visible TCP, UDP, and Unix-domain sockets are printed in a table. Address
family, protocol, listening state, process identifiers, and the header can be
selected with ss-compatible options. The \fB\-\-live\fR option, which has no
short form, refreshes the table
until interrupted. Its optional value is the number of seconds between
refreshes, and defaults to 0.5.
.TP
.B goodstat
File metadata is printed as a labeled report. The \fB\-L\fR option follows
symbolic links. The \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-f\fR options add a CRC32C checksum and
filesystem capacity report, respectively.
.TP
.B goodfsw
Path changes are reported by polling filesystem metadata. Machine-readable
output is the default, and each record contains the Unix scan time, a numeric
event mask, and the path. The \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-human-readable\fR options use
formatted timestamps and descriptive event labels, and \fB\-m\fR and
\fB\-\-machine-readable\fR select the numeric format explicitly. The
\fB\-\-timezone\fR option selects local or UTC time for human output, and
\fB\-\-precision\fR selects the fractional timestamp digits. The \fB\-r\fR,
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-event-flags\fR, \fB\-x\fR, \fB\-1\fR, \fB\-l\fR, and
\fB\-e\fR options select recursion, timestamp prefixes, appended event labels,
one filesystem, one event batch, the polling period, and an excluded substring.
.TP
.B retry
A command is repeated until it succeeds or its attempt limit is reached. The
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-b\fR, \fB\-m\fR, and \fB\-q\fR options set the
attempt count, initial delay, backoff factor, maximum delay, and quiet mode.
.TP
.B stat
File or filesystem metadata is printed. The \fB\-L\fR, \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-t\fR,
\fB\-c\fR, and \fB\-\-printf\fR options dereference links, select filesystem
data, select terse output, apply a format, and apply a format without an
automatic newline.
.TP
.B sync
Dirty filesystem data is flushed. The \fB\-d\fR option flushes file data for
each operand and needs one. The \fB\-f\fR option flushes the filesystem
containing each operand, and every filesystem without one.
.TP
.B watch
A command is run at a fixed interval. The \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-t\fR, \fB\-g\fR, and
\fB\-e\fR options set the interval, omit the title, exit after output changes,
and exit after a command error. The \fB\-x\fR option is accepted for
compatibility.
.PP
Utilities with styled reports use color when their output stream is a terminal.
Redirected output contains no terminal escape sequences.
.TP
.B wc
Lines, words, characters, and bytes are counted with \fB\-l\fR, \fB\-w\fR,
\fB\-m\fR, and \fB\-c\fR.
.TP
.B which
Builtins, PATH programs, and koshkit utilities are resolved. The \fB\-a\fR,
\fB\-\-all\fR, \fB\-q\fR, and \fB\-\-quiet\fR options are supported.
.TP
.B whoami
The effective user name is printed.
.TP
.B yes
The joined operands, or y when none are given, are repeated until output
closes.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode.
.B set \-o vi
selects vi mode and
.B set \-o emacs
restores emacs mode.
.PP
Input taller than the terminal shows only the rows around the caret, leaving
two rows for hints, and the shown rows follow the caret as it moves. The ghost
suggestion and the right prompt are not drawn while the input is that tall.
.PP
With a ghost suggestion at the end of the line, the right arrow and
End accept all of it. Ctrl\-Right and Alt\-F accept only its next word.
.PP
Ctrl\-K, Ctrl\-U, Ctrl\-W, Alt\-D, Alt\-Backspace and Ctrl\-Delete save the
erased text in a kill ring of 16 entries that lasts for the session. Consecutive
kills join into one entry. Ctrl\-K on an empty line kills the newline after it,
so the next line moves up to the cursor. On an empty last line, Ctrl\-K removes
the line and moves the cursor to the end of the previous line. Ctrl\-Y inserts
the newest entry, and Alt\-Y right after a yank replaces it with the next older
entry. Alt\-. and Alt\-_ insert the last word of the newest history entry, and each
further press uses an older entry.
Undo reverts a yank or a last-word insertion with all of its replacements in
one step.
.PP
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E opens the line in a temporary file with the editor selected by
VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi. When the editor exits with status 0, the saved text
replaces the line, and nothing runs until Enter is pressed. If the editor is
not found or exits with another status, the line is unchanged and a message
appears above the prompt.
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-U undoes the last edit. The Ctrl\-X chords work in emacs mode
only, and the vi command
.B :e
opens the line in the editor in their place.
.PP
A key waiting for another key, such as Ctrl\-X or a vi operator, shows the keys
that complete it on the hint rows below the input until the next key arrives.
Turning off both
.B editor.hints.show_command_synopsis
and
.B editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics
turns these rows off too.
.PP
Ctrl\-Z undoes while the editor collects a line, the same as Ctrl\-_. In a foreground program, Ctrl\-Z stops it and the shell reports the stopped
job. Ctrl\-Shift\-Z redoes when the terminal
reports it apart from Ctrl\-Z. While the editor collects a line, it requests
such reports through the kitty keyboard protocol and xterm modifyOtherKeys. Inside tmux, the
modifyOtherKeys request takes effect when the tmux
.B extended\-keys
option is on. The
.B koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off
command stops the requests from the next prompt on. The option is on by
default. Without these modes most terminals send the Ctrl\-Z byte for both
keys. On the Windows console both keys undo and Ctrl\-^ redoes.
.PP
A control character in the line, a ghost suggestion, or a menu entry is drawn
in caret notation, such as ^[ for ESC. A filename that holds a control
character completes in the $'...' form with escapes.
.PP
On a non-empty line, the up arrow recalls only the history entries beginning
with the text typed before the first up arrow, and the down arrow moves back.
An empty line steps through the whole history. The
.B koshconf set editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix off
command turns this off. The option is on by default.
.PP
Once the cursor is past the command word, the rows directly below the input
explain the command under the cursor under a
.B synopsis
header. Each of up to 16 rows pairs a word of the command with its
meaning: the command itself, a subcommand such as
.BR "git commit" ,
and each flag of the command, before or after the cursor. The word under the
cursor is marked with
.BR > ,
and a command with more rows shows the ones nearest the cursor.
A command is described by its manual page, builtin or koshkit help, alias
definition, or function location, and by its synopsis when it has no
description. The rows are hidden while a completion menu or history search is
open. A keystroke never runs
.B man
or the command.
.PP
After a short pause, the hint of an external program loads its section 1
manual page through
.BR man ,
a subcommand page such as
.BR git\-commit (1),
and, for a command on the help allowlist, the output of its help flag. The
trust rules of flag completion apply, so
.BR man ,
.BR manpath ,
and the program run only from an absolute directory owned by the user or root
that no group or other user can write. A program found through an empty or
relative PATH element never runs. A Tab that needs a load shows a
.B loading...
row while it waits. The
.B koshconf set editor.hints.show_command_synopsis off
command turns these hints off and stops the loads. The option is on by
default.
.PP
Targets for
.B make
are completed from the Makefile, and targets after
.B "cmake \-\-build"
.I dir
.B \-\-target
from
.IR dir /CMakeFiles/TargetDirectories.txt.
Neither
.B make
nor
.B cmake
is started, and no $(shell ...) function runs.
.PP
In the same pause, a line that parses is analyzed as Enter would analyze it in
the current mood and warning level. The rows show the first finding under its
severity,
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
or
.BR note ,
in place of the explanation. This analysis follows no sourced file, resolves no
command, opens no file, and searches no PATH. The
.B koshconf set editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics off
command turns off the finding and the syntax problem below. The option is on by
default and is independent of
.BR editor.hints.show_command_synopsis .
.PP
When running the line would report a syntax problem, the rows show the first
parse error and its detail under
.B "syntax error"
in place of the explanation. The editor submits the whole buffer on Enter and
has no continuation prompt, so an open construct is an error even when a later
line would close it. A line over 2048 bytes shows nothing.
.PP
The
.B koshconf set editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes on
command makes a typed
.BR ( ,
.BR [ ,
.BR { ,
double quote, or single quote insert its closer after the caret. Typing a
closer over an inserted one steps over it, and Backspace between an empty
inserted pair deletes both. The option is off by default.
.PP
The interactive terminal title shows the current user and directory, or the
command line of the running foreground program. Output from
PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can replace the idle title.
.PP
RPS1, or RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt. It is expanded
with PS1 before each prompt and drawn right-aligned on the first input row. It
is hidden when the row has no room for it or when its expansion holds a control
character other than a color sequence. It is never part of the submitted line.
.PP
A nonempty KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1 makes Enter redraw the submitted line, and
Ctrl\-C redraw the interrupted line before its ^C, with its expansion in place
of PS1. KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 does the same for the right prompt. See both
variables under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
Ghost text uses prefix matches from completion and history. Tab also offers
smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command position includes
keywords, builtins, functions, aliases, koshkit utilities, and programs from
PATH. A directory candidate always ends in a slash. The operand of
.B cd
or
.B pushd
completes only directories, and when it is neither absolute nor led by a dot or
a tilde, also the directories under each CDPATH entry.
.PP
An explicit Tab first replaces the token with the longest common prefix. A sole
candidate replaces the token and takes the space the setting below asks for.
When more than one candidate remains, the selector chosen by
.B \-\-tab\-selector
or completion.menu_style presents the candidates. The
completion.add_space_after_completed_word setting chooses whether an accepted
complete candidate takes one space after it:
.B off
never, the default,
.B on
always, and
.B on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash
unless the candidate ends in a slash.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu of the candidates below the line. The
down arrow moves to the next row, the up arrow moves back, and Tab accepts the
highlighted row. In the completion menu, Escape closes the menu with the
current edit, Ctrl\-G restores the line that opened it, and Enter closes the
menu and runs the line as it stands, without accepting the highlighted row. A
first row shows the active source and the available keys, labeled
.B selecting completions
for the candidates of a token and
.B incremental history search
for a snapshot of the shared history file. Opening the search does not
replace or merge the session's private history.
.PP
A printable key is inserted into the token, and the menu is drawn again from the
candidates that still match. A command, path, or koshconf option list
keeps the best of three tiers: the candidates the token opens exactly, then
those it opens in smart case, then those holding the bytes of an identifier-like
token in order. Any other completion list keeps every candidate the token opens,
comparing letters without case. In the history search, an entry matches when the
query opens it, appears inside it whole, or appears inside it as bytes in
order. Backspace erases the last character of the token and widens the list. A
key that leaves the listed candidates, such as a blank, a slash, or an erase
below the token, asks the completion again once typing has paused for 24
milliseconds. An accepted candidate referring to a directory opens a menu of
the entries it holds. Any other key closes the menu and then acts on the line.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to the program given by
.B KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND
and its output replaces the token. When a selector reports no match or is
dismissed, the line is left alone. A single remaining candidate keeps the plain
listing, and so does a session without a controlling terminal. An empty
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ,
a command resolving to no program, and a selector exiting with a status
other than 0, 1, or 130 each report a message above the prompt and keep the
plain listing.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates below the prompt as a static column
list. A terminal without decoration support always receives the plain listing.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the same selector. The line is the search
query, and the matching entries are offered newest first. An accepted row
replaces the whole line. The plain selector keeps the incremental search inside
the editor and accepts the keys listed under History search keys. An empty
history leaves the line alone.
.PP
When the caret is on a parenthesis, bracket, or brace, or just after one, that
bracket and its partner are highlighted. A bracket inside quotes, a comment, or
a here-document body, or escaped with a backslash, never pairs.
.PP
Each vi line begins in insert mode. Escape enters command mode. Command mode
supports motions, counted operators, lettered registers, change repeat, and
visual selection.
.PP
In command mode, arrow keys move between buffer rows without recalling history.
Insert-mode arrow keys recall history.
.PP
The subsections below list every key the editor binds. A key not listed for a
mode does nothing there unless its subsection says otherwise. An unrecognized
key is a control key or an escape sequence that acts as no key at all, such as
Ctrl\-G, Ctrl\-O, a bare Escape, or a function key.
.SS Emacs mode keys
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-A ", " Home
Move to the start of the current line of the buffer.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-E ", " End
Move to the end of the current line, or accept the whole ghost suggestion at
the buffer end.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Home ", " Ctrl\-End
Move to the start or the end of the buffer.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-B ", " Left
Move one character left.
.TP
.B Right
Move one character right, or accept the ghost suggestion at the buffer end.
.TP
.BR Alt\-B ", " Ctrl\-Left ", " Alt\-Left
Move to the start of the previous word.
.TP
.BR Alt\-F ", " Ctrl\-Right ", " Alt\-Right
Move past the next word, or accept the next word of the ghost suggestion.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-P ", " Up
Move up a row, or recall an older history entry from the first row. A buffer of
several lines that was typed or edited, and joins no line with a backslash,
takes a second press before it is left for history.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-N ", " Down
Move down a row, or recall a newer history entry from the last row.
.TP
