.SS Vi colon commands
The colon key in command mode opens a command line under the buffer. Enter
runs it, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or Backspace on an empty command closes
it. Only a substitution, an undo, a redo, a format, and the external editor
change the line, and only the option commands change the settings. Opened from
a visual selection, the command line acts on the selected lines. The buffer
keeps its syntax highlighting while the command line is open.
.TP
.BR :q ", " :q! ", " :quit ", " :wq ", " :wq! ", " :wqa ", " :wqa! ", " :x
Leave the shell with the status of the last command. In
.BR slurp ,
the w forms save the file first and leave only when the save succeeds.
.TP
.BR :w ", " :write
In
.BR slurp ,
save the file and keep editing.
.TP
.BI :s/ old / new / \fR[\fBg\fR]
Replace the first match of the basic regular expression
.I old
on the row of the caret with
.IR new ,
or every match with
.BR g .
.B :%s
acts on every row of the buffer. Any punctuation byte can replace the slash,
and a backslash keeps the delimiter literal. In
.IR new ,
.B &
stands for the match and
.B \e1
through
.B \e9
for its groups. The substitution is one undo step.
.TP
.BR :& ", " :&& ", " :s
Repeat the last substitution on the row of the caret,
.B :&&
with its
.B g
flag and the others without it.
.B :%&
and
.B :%&&
act on every row.
.TP
.BI ":set " option = value
Set an option of
.BR koshconf .
.BI ":set " option ?
shows its value. A bare
.I option
turns a boolean option on and shows any other one, and
.BI no option
turns a boolean option off. Tab completes the option, and the hint rows list
the options that match it. The result appears on the hint rows until the next
key.
.TP
.BR :save " [\fIoption\fR]"
Write the current value of
.I option
to
.IR kosh.conf ,
or every option when none is given.
.TP
.BR :format ", " :%format
Format the row of the caret, or the whole buffer, the way
.B \-\-format
does, as one undo step. A source that does not parse stays as it is, and its
first error appears on the hint rows.
.TP
.BR :e ", " :edit
Open the line in the editor selected by VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi, as Ctrl\-X
Ctrl\-E does in emacs mode.
.TP
