.SS Terminal shell integration
An interactive shell with terminal standard output writes semantic marks that
terminals use to find prompts, commands, exit statuses, and the working
directory. The marks are the OSC 133 sequences A, B, C, and D and the OSC 7
working directory sequence. When TERM_PROGRAM is vscode, the shell also writes
OSC 633 marks for VS Code. No mark is written when standard output is not a
terminal, when TERM is dumb, or when
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
is 0.
.SH JOB CONTROL
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job. POSIX stages share
one process group. The final stage determines $!, the ordinary pipeline status,
and the primary job identity. With pipefail, the rightmost failing stage may
determine the status.
.PP
In the bash, bash-posix, and sh moods an asynchronous simple command or
assignment forks before expanding its words, prefix assignments, and
redirections, as Bash and dash do. An expansion error then fails the job, with
status 1 in the bash moods and 2 in the sh mood. The kosh mood expands the words
in the shell before starting the job.
.PP
An ampersand ends a whole and-or list in every mood. In
.B a && b || c &
the entire list runs in one background job, and its variable and directory
changes stay in the job.
.PP
A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the final one. The
.B wait
builtin returns that stored status. The
.B fg
and
.B bg
builtins resume every stage of the job.
.PP
.B jobs
and the notification of a finished or stopped job print one line per job in
the columns Bash uses: the job number and its current or previous mark, the
process with
.BR \-l ,
the state, and the command. A job ended by a signal shows the description of
the signal, such as Terminated or Killed, and wait still reports 128 plus the
signal number. A job that exited shows Done, or Exit and its status.
.PP
A job reported by
.BR wait ,
.BR jobs ,
or a notification leaves the job table, and a later
.B wait
with that process returns the remembered status again. The shell remembers the 1024 most recent statuses.
.B wait
reports 127 and a diagnostic for a process that is not a child of the shell or
a job that does not exist. With several operands, the last operand decides the
status.
.PP
A subshell, a pipeline stage, an asynchronous command, and a coprocess cannot
wait for a job of their parent. They report 127 for its process or number. In
the sh mood a job number matching no job reports 2 from
.B wait
and
.BR jobs .
.PP
.B wait \-n
returns the status of the next job to finish among the operand jobs and
processes, or among every job when no operand is given. It reports 127
when no operand or tracked job is left.
.B wait \-p
.I var
stores the process whose status is returned in
.IR var ,
and unsets it when no status is returned. A stopped job returns 128 plus the
stop signal, and
.B wait \-n
skips it.
.B wait \-f
waits until each job terminates.
.PP
A coprocess is one asynchronous job and sets $!. Its descriptors are numbered
above 9 and are closed across an exec. A subshell receives neither descriptor.
Once the coprocess has finished, the shell closes its remaining descriptors and
unsets NAME and NAME_PID, as Bash does.
.SH SIGNALS
The main shell ignores SIGPIPE. A forked child restores the default action, so
a producer terminated by SIGPIPE returns status 141.
.PP
On Windows, a background process runs in its own console process group.
.PP
The CHLD trap runs its action once for every child the shell reaps, at the next
command boundary. Under
.B set -m
a job that stops counts as one arrival of its own.
.PP
When a trapped signal arrives while
.B wait
blocks, the wait ends and
.B wait
reports 128 plus the signal number. The awaited job is left running.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors, warnings, and analysis diagnostics include source locations when a
mapping exists.
.PP
After a parse error, the shell skips only the failed statement and keeps
parsing the command list around it. Lint, the language server, and a script
report every syntax error in one run. A construct that a mood does not provide,
such as
.B [[
in the sh mood, is reported, and the command parses as usual.
.PP
Analysis follows static source operands recursively when the files can be read.
It skips dynamic operands and unavailable files.
.PP
In the default mood, strict and lenient findings reject the script. Annoying
findings remain warnings.
.PP
In the default mood, a script is rejected when it expands a variable it never
assigns, calls a function that uses arguments without passing any, or calls a
function before defining it.
.PP
An assignment in a function to a variable not declared
.B local
is reported. In the
.B sh
mood there is no
.BR local ,
so only a variable first set inside a function and also used outside it
is reported.
.PP
A comment of the form
.B # shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted-command-substitution
suppresses the listed analysis checks. A numeric code suppresses every variant
under that code. A slug suppresses only its own variant. The value may be set to
.BR all ,
or contain a range such as
.BR SC2000-SC2100 .
A directive before the first command applies to the complete file. A later
directive applies to the next complete and-or command. These comments do not
suppress parser or runtime diagnostics.
.PP
A diagnostic gives the file, line, and column of the failing command, quotes
the source line, and puts a caret under it. Text built at run time, such as an
eval string or a trap action, is its own source. A location in an eval string
appears as
.BR eval:2:7: ,
counted from the start of that string. A location in a trap action appears as
.BR trap:1:5: .
A
.B \-c
string is labeled
.BR \-c ,
and a location in it appears as
.BR \-c:1:5: .
Standard input and interactive lines have no label.
.PP
Each level of indirection between the top level and the failing command adds
one backtrace frame. The levels are a function call, a sourced file, an eval,
a trap action, a subshell, and a command, function, or process substitution. A
diagnostic with no indirection has no frame.
.PP
A frame begins with
.BR trace: .
A repeated frame is printed once and its header ends with the repeat count.
When several
.B \-c
strings or files are given, the frame that points into the command line is
printed once, after all diagnostics from that string or file. If the shell
exits or replaces itself before those diagnostics end, the frame is printed
first.
Diagnostics print the same text in every mood.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
On Windows an environment variable holds at most 32,766 UTF-16 units, and a
child process receives a longer value cut to that limit.
.TP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
The value supplies default command-line options.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANALYSIS
The variable passes the analysis settings changed with
.B set
to launched shells. Each use of
.B set
rewrites it from the current state as whitespace-separated words:
.B mimicry
for
.BR \-I ,
.BI warnings= N
for the level of
.BR \-W ,
.B no\-annoying
for optimizer.show_annoying_tier off, and
.B no\-diagnostics
for optimizer.analyze_before_running off.
The variable is removed when no word remains. A shell loads it at startup. An
explicit
.B \-W
level replaces the inherited level.
.TP
.B KOSH_DEBUG_SYSTEM_KOSHCONF
A non-empty value selects the system configuration file in place of
/etc/kosh.conf or %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf, with the same ownership check.
Only a debug build honors it.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
The value specifies the command history file. The default is ~/.kosh_history.
A value inherited from the environment overrides editor.history.file_path in
the settings files. Like bash's HISTFILE, it is not exported unless inherited
or exported explicitly. HISTFILE itself has no effect on the history file.
A record longer than 65535 bytes is skipped when the file is read, and the
other records stay available.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE
The value limits the number of history events retained for recall, listing, and
storage. The default is 4096, and zero disables retention and storage. A value
inherited from the environment overrides editor.history.max_entries in the
settings files, and it is exported only when inherited or exported explicitly.
.TP
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
An interactive shell on a terminal marks each prompt, command, exit status, and
working directory with OSC 133 and OSC 7 sequences understood by terminals and
IDE terminals. A value of 0 turns the marks off. They are not written when
standard output is not a terminal or TERM is dumb. See
.BR "Terminal shell integration" .
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR
These variables select the editor used by
.BR fc .
FCEDIT takes precedence over EDITOR.
.TP
.B KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The value is the file of directories visited, which z ranks by frequency and
recency. The default is
~/.kosh_directory_history.
.TP
.B KOSH_CALC_HISTORY
The value specifies the interactive calc history file. The default is
~/.kosh_calc_history.
.TP
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ", " KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS
These variables configure the external tab selector.
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND specifies the program receiving the candidates
and defaults to
.BR fzf .
KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS holds the presentation options and defaults to
\-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=~40% \-\-min\-height=3. The value is
split on blanks with no expansion.
.TP
.BR ENV ", " BASH_ENV
These variables select startup files under the conditions in
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.TP
.B PATH
The value is a colon-separated command search path. An empty element, an
empty value, and an unset PATH search the current directory. On Windows the
separator is a semicolon, and an empty element is skipped. The
.B \-\-no\-init\-files
option resets it to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " CDPATH
HOME supplies home paths. PWD and OLDPWD record the logical current and prior
directories. CDPATH supplies search directories for a relative
.B cd
operand. A relative directory is resolved against the logical PWD. The form
.B cd ..
removes the last logical component, unless
.B cd \-P
or the physical option resolves symbolic links first.
.B cd \-L
keeps the logical form. A logical PWD is accepted only when it is absolute and
identifies the physical current directory.
.TP
.BR SHELL ", " SHLVL
SHELL specifies the executable. The invocation path is used when SHELL is
unset. A child shell inherits SHLVL and increments it.
.TP
.B IFS
The value controls field splitting and the join performed by "$*". The default
contains space, tab, and newline.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
An interactive shell stores the initial terminal width and height in these
variables.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
The command runs before each interactive prompt. When the variable is an
indexed array, each nonempty element runs as a command in index order. Every
element sees the status and $_ of the previous command, and neither the status
nor $_ of a hook replaces them. PROMPT_COMMAND is deprecated and discouraged.
The prompt escapes cover its usual uses without running a command: \\g inserts
the git branch, \\L the duration of the last command, and \\M its memory use.
.TP
.BR PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4
These variables provide the pre-execution, primary, continuation, select, and
xtrace prompts. PS0 defaults to empty. PS2 defaults to "> ". PS3 defaults to
"#? ". PS4 defaults to "+ ". The first byte of PS4 is repeated once per
enclosing command substitution, process substitution, eval, source, and trap
action. The default PS1 shows the git branch with its ahead and
behind counts, then the user, host, and working directory, and it ends with a
bullet.
The shell expands the backslash escapes \\u,
\\h, \\H, \\k, \\K, \\w, \\W, \\P, \\g, \\$, \\?, \\j, \\L, \\M, \\., \\t, \\T,
\\@, \\A, \\d, \\D{format}, \\s, \\v, \\V, \\!, \\#, \\l, \\n, \\r, \\e, \\a,
\\[, \\], \\\\, and \\nnn. The \\nnn escape inserts the byte with the octal
value nnn, from one to three digits.
The \\k and \\K escapes insert the hostname as \\h and \\H do, colored when
standard output is a color terminal. The \\s escape inserts the last component
of the path the shell was started with, without a leading login dash or an .exe
suffix. The \\v escape inserts the major and minor version and \\V adds the
patch. The \\! escape inserts the history number the next command receives. The
\\# escape inserts the command number, which starts at 1 and increases by one
for each command line taken from a terminal, a script file, or standard input.
The \\l escape inserts the last component of the terminal device of standard
input, such as 3 for /dev/pts/3, and tty when standard input is not a terminal.
The \\D{format} escape inserts the time formatted by strftime, as Bash does. The
\\L escape inserts the duration of the last command, empty when it took under 5
milliseconds. The \\M escape inserts the resident memory that the last command
used: the highest peak among the processes that the command started and their
descendants, or the growth of the shell's own memory during the command when
that is larger. The escape is empty after a command that only reuses memory the
shell already holds. Text inserted by an escape is never expanded.
A raw 001 or 002 byte in PS0, PS1, RPS1, KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1, or
KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 delimits an invisible region the way \\[ and \\] do.
The \\. escape emits a bullet colored by the last exit status. It is white
after status 0 and bold red after status 1. On a truecolor terminal a higher
status shifts the red toward magenta in four steps, and other terminals use
bold red for it. The user, host, directory, branch, shell name, and
version inserted by the escapes draw a control byte in caret notation, such as
^[ for escape, and a byte that is not UTF-8 as \\xHH.
.TP
.BR RPS1 ", " RPROMPT
RPS1 is the right prompt drawn at the right edge of the first input row.
RPROMPT is used when RPS1 is unset or empty. The value expands like PS1. When
an interactive shell starts with neither variable set, RPS1 defaults to a dim
"\\L \\M", the duration and memory use of the last command. Spaces at either
end of the expanded right prompt are dropped. An empty escape leaves no gap.
See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1
A nonempty value expands like PS1 and replaces the prompt on a line that Enter
submits or Ctrl\-C interrupts. For example, KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1='\\$ ' leaves
"$ ", or "# " for root, before each command in the scrollback. When it is unset
or empty, the line keeps its prompt. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1
A nonempty value expands like RPS1 and replaces the right prompt on a line that
Enter submits or Ctrl\-C interrupts. When it is unset or empty, the line keeps
its right prompt. An interactive shell that starts with it unset sets it to
\\[\\], which erases the right prompt. For example, KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1='\\L'
leaves the duration of each command beside the next line. See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The value selects the xtrace descriptor. Standard error is used when it is unset
or invalid.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " COLORTERM ", " NO_COLOR
TERM selects terminal capabilities on POSIX systems. When COLORTERM contains
truecolor or 24bit, the syntax, file, diagnostic, and koshkit colors are
24-bit colors. When TERM contains 256color or direct, they are 256-color
shades, and any other terminal draws the basic colors. A nonempty NO_COLOR
disables prompt, diagnostic, koshkit cat, and editor color.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Completion searches these manual paths for subcommands and options.
.TP
.B OPTERR
A value of zero silences the diagnostics of
.BR getopts .
A leading colon in the option string silences them too.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The value specifies the directory used for a temporary file. The default is
/tmp.
.TP
.BR PIPESTATUS ", " REPLY ", " OPTIND ", " OPTARG ", " TIMEFORMAT
These variables hold pipeline statuses, default read and select input, getopts
state, and time formatting.
.TP
.BR KOSH ", " KOSH_VERSION ", " KOSH_COMMIT ", " KOSH_BUILD_MODE ", " KOSH_OS
These values identify the invocation path, version, build commit, build mode,
and build host distribution and kernel. The shell never exports them. A value
supplied by the environment is replaced and loses its export attribute.
.TP
.B KOSH_GIT_BRANCH
The value is the branch of the repository holding the working directory, a
short commit hash for a detached HEAD, and empty outside a repository. An
assignment overrides detection. The search never enters a directory listed in
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES.
.TP
.BR KOSH_GIT_AHEAD ", " KOSH_GIT_BEHIND
The values contain the unpushed and unpulled commit counts relative to the
current branch's upstream. Both are empty outside a repository, when no upstream
is configured, and when the count is zero.
.TP
.BR KOSH_PS1_ *
Each variable expands to the current text of one prompt escape. Assignments to
these variables are ignored. The variables are KOSH_PS1_USER for \\u,
KOSH_PS1_HOST for \\h, KOSH_PS1_HOST_FULL for \\H, KOSH_PS1_CWD for \\w,
KOSH_PS1_CWD_BASE for \\W, KOSH_PS1_CWD_SHORT for \\P, KOSH_PS1_GIT_BRANCH for
\\g, KOSH_PS1_PROMPT_SIGN for \\$, KOSH_PS1_STATUS for \\?, KOSH_PS1_JOBS for \\j,
KOSH_PS1_DURATION for \\L, KOSH_PS1_MEMORY for \\M, KOSH_PS1_DOT for \\.,
KOSH_PS1_TIME for \\t, KOSH_PS1_TIME_12 for \\T, KOSH_PS1_TIME_AMPM for \\@,
KOSH_PS1_TIME_SHORT for \\A, KOSH_PS1_DATE for \\d, KOSH_PS1_SHELL for \\s,
KOSH_PS1_VERSION for \\v, KOSH_PS1_VERSION_FULL for \\V, KOSH_PS1_TTY for \\l,
KOSH_PS1_HISTORY_NUMBER for \\!, and KOSH_PS1_COMMAND_NUMBER for \\#.
.TP
.B KOSH_IDENTITY
The read-only exported value is the lowercase CRC-32 identity of the running
executable. An inherited value is discarded.
.TP
.B KOSHCONF
Expanding the variable supplies the mood and the interactive options in the
base64 binary form that
.BR kosh (5)
describes. An assignment is discarded, and the shell never exports it by
itself. A KOSHCONF value in the environment at startup is applied after the
configuration files and removed from the environment. For example,
.B ssh host KOSHCONF=\(dq$KOSHCONF\(dq kosh
carries the settings to a remote shell.
.B koshconf load
applies a value to the current session.
.TP
.BR KOSH_WELCOME ", " KOSH_FAREWELL
A value is printed once at interactive startup (KOSH_WELCOME) or exit
(KOSH_FAREWELL), prefixed with the shell name. An unset value or a value of
zero display width prints nothing. An interactive shell sets both to a
greeting and a farewell unless the environment holds them, so an exported
empty value disables the message.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANSI_*
The variables BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their
BRIGHT_ forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET supply terminal escapes. A recognized
variable is empty when color is disabled. An explicit assignment takes precedence.
.TP
.BR RANDOM ", " SRANDOM ", " SECONDS ", " EPOCHSECONDS ", " EPOCHREALTIME
These dynamic values supply random numbers, elapsed time, and wall clock time.
An assignment to
.B SECONDS
moves the base of the elapsed count. An assignment to
.B RANDOM
seeds the generator, and a repeated seed repeats the sequence. An
.B unset
of
.B SECONDS
or
.B RANDOM
makes the variable ordinary for the rest of the shell. These variables are
unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B LINENO
The line number of the running command is available in every mood. Inside a
command substitution, backticks, or a process substitution, it is the file line
of the command expanding it.
.TP
.BR BASHPID ", " PPID ", " UID ", " EUID ", " GROUPS ", " HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
These dynamic values identify the current shell process, parent, ids,
groups, host, and platform. The value of $$ remains the original shell process
id. These variables are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B GLOBIGNORE
The value lists colon\-separated patterns that pathname expansion removes from
its results. Assignment enables
.B dotglob
and unsetting disables it. The pathname expansion section describes the
matching.
.TP
.B HISTCMD
The value is the history number of the current command. A non-interactive shell
reports zero. An assignment is discarded. The variable is unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR SHELLOPTS ", " BASHOPTS ", " BASH_SUBSHELL ", " BASH_ARGV0 ", " BASH_EXECUTION_STRING ", " BASH_MONOSECONDS
These values expose set options, shopt options, subshell depth, argument zero,
the current command string, and a monotonic millisecond count. The two option
lists are read-only. These variables are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR COPROC ", " COPROC_PID
The
.B coproc
command binds these to the descriptors and the process id of a coprocess
given no NAME. A coprocess given a NAME binds NAME and NAME_PID and leaves
these unset. These variables are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_ALIASES
This associative array contains the current aliases. Assigning an element
defines or replaces that alias. Unsetting an element has no effect. Unsetting
the whole variable disconnects the array without deleting the aliases. The
variable is unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR BASH_ARGC ", " BASH_ARGV
.B BASH_ARGC
contains the positional parameter count for each recorded call frame.
.B BASH_ARGV
contains the recorded arguments in reverse order, with the innermost frame
first. The root shell is the last frame. Changing positional parameters does
not change arguments already recorded for a frame.
.PP
The
.B extdebug
shopt option records function calls and sourced-file calls. Without
.BR extdebug ,
function calls are not recorded. Assignments and declarations with values have
no effect. Unset and local declarations fail. The variables are unavailable in
the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B DIRSTACK
This indexed array contains the current directory at index zero followed by the
saved directories that
.B dirs
prints. Assigning index zero or an index past the end has no effect. Assigning
another existing index changes that saved stack entry. Unsetting an element has
no effect. Unsetting the whole variable disconnects the array without clearing
the directory stack. The variable is unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR FUNCNAME ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_COMMAND
These values describe the active call stack and command. Their array forms
contain the complete stack. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The array contains groups from the last [[ ... =~ ... ]] match.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
These values report a Bash identity. They are initialized when the runtime mood
or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR COMP_WORDS ", " COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDBREAKS ", " COMPREPLY
A programmable completion function uses these values and writes COMPREPLY.
COMP_WORDS contains the words on the line. COMP_CWORD is the index of the word
under the cursor. COMP_LINE contains the complete line. COMP_POINT is the
cursor byte offset. COMP_WORDBREAKS contains the bytes that split a word.
Trailing spaces are removed from each COMPREPLY entry. The
completion.add_space_after_completed_word option and the nospace option
of the completion spec control the space after an accepted candidate. The spec's
.B \-X
filter and its
.B \-P
and
.B \-S
text apply to COMPREPLY entries. With
.B complete -o filenames
or
.BR "compopt -o filenames" ,
a slash is appended to each entry that refers to a directory.
.B compopt +o filenames
turns the slash off for the current completion.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran. A command path that
is a directory, or has a trailing separator and is not a directory, returns
126. A script file that cannot be opened returns 127. A syntax error in a
script or a
.B \-c
string returns 2, and so does an
.B eval
or a sourced file whose text does not parse. A usage error in a
non-rescue invocation returns 2. In the bash and bash-posix moods, an
expansion error that ends the shell, such as an unset parameter under nounset,
a
.B ${name:?}
operand, or a bad substitution, returns 127 from a
.B \-c
string, as Bash does, and 1 from a script file or standard input. A child
terminated by SIGPIPE returns 141. A
.B \-\-lint
batch returns 1 when an input cannot be read or parsed, or its analysis reports
an error. Warning-only findings return 0.
The
.B \-\-format
mode returns 1 when an input cannot be read, parsed, formatted, or replaced.
The
.B \-\-apply
mode also returns 1 when a safe replacement cannot be completed. These modes
return 0 after every requested input succeeds. An invalid option combination
returns 2.
.SH ERRORS
An unavailable command or pathname is reported through its first unavailable
component when that component can be identified.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_history
This is the default command history file.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_directory_history
This is the default file of directories visited for z.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_calc_history
This is the default interactive calc history file.
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf ", " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf ", " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
These are the system and user settings files. Windows loads the system file
from %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf.
.PP
The settings files and the shell startup files are described in
.BR kosh (5).
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B kosh
and
.B bash
moods accept Bash syntax in addition to POSIX syntax. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled. The
.B sh
mood uses the supported dash-compatible behavior.
.PP
The 0b arithmetic prefix is a kosh extension. Bash writes the same value with
a 2# prefix. The
.BR enable ,
.BR bind ,
and selected
.B help
flags are accepted compatibility forms with the effects described under
.BR BUILTINS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (5),
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
